  "raw": {
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11262?c=bc": {
      "title": "Administrative Office Temp Pool",
      "description": "Administrative Office Temp Pool | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Administrative Office Temp Pool | req11262 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Position Details: Location: Chestnut Hill, MA – Onsite Length of Assignment: Varies based on assignment and hours per week worked Hours: 5–30 hours per week, depending on availability and demand Hourly Range: $24 - $26/hour. Salary commensurate with relevant experience. Administrative Office Temp Pool The Administrative Office Temp Pool provides temporary administrative, secretarial, and office support across various departments. Individuals in this pool may be assigned to short-term or long-term placements based on organizational needs.  The length of an assignment is usually determined at the time a request is made by the department, but it can extend beyond or end prior to the original request. Key Responsibilities: Perform general administrative and clerical duties, including data entry, document preparation, and record-keeping. Utilize office technology and software to manage files, compile reports, and process information. Organize and maintain physical and digital records for easy retrieval. Greet and assist visitors, students, and staff, providing information and directing inquiries appropriately. Handle incoming and outgoing mail distribution. Assist with scheduling, calendar management, and office communications. Operate office equipment, including phones, printers, copiers, and scanners. Provide customer service and engage in public-facing interactions to share or clarify information about department activities. Requirements Strong organizational and multitasking abilities. Proficiency in office software (e.g., Microsoft Office Suite, Google Workspace). Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to multi-task and have good follow through Ability to adapt to different work environments and team dynamics. Attention to detail and the ability to handle confidential information professionally. Assignments in this temp pool vary in length and scope, offering a flexible work environment for individuals with administrative skills and experience. Closing Statement Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11317?c=bc": {
      "title": "Tutor - Master's in Data Science (Temporary)",
      "description": "Tutor - Master's in Data Science (Temporary) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Tutor - Master's in Data Science (Temporary) Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11317 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Tutor - Master's in Data\nScience (Temporary) The online Master's in Data Science is a 36-credit program\nhoused within the Measurement, Evaluation, Statistics, and Assessment\nDepartment at the Lynch School of Education and Human Development. In line with\nthe Jesuit values at the core of Boston College, the program ensures graduates\n(a) approach data science with a human-centered mindset, which will prepare\ngraduates to place people and society at the forefront; (b) instill in students\nthe importance of ethical and responsible data use; and (c) ensure that equity\nand the minimization of biases are at the center of their training in data\nscience, from data collection, processing, storage, to modeling, presentation,\nand interpretation. The online M.S. in Data Science program at Boston College is seeking Tutors to\nsupport our courses during the upcoming semester. This role is expected to\naverage 10-15 hours per week across the semester, with a maximum of 170 hours. Please\nnote that the stated hours per week represent the maximum number of hours\npermitted for this role, and additional hours will not be available. The Tutor role offers valuable experience in teaching and\nmentoring at the graduate level in a fast-growing program. Tutors play a valued\nrole in our program, supporting our students and faculty with excellence and\nkindness. Courses requiring Tutors: Spring 2027 semester: (beginning of January through mid-May) Applied Math for Data Science General Linear Models Deep Learning & AI Capstone in Applied Data Science I We are also interested in hiring tutors for the\nfollowing semesters: Summer semester: (beginning of May through mid-Aug) Data Visualization & Storytelling Multivariate Statistical Analysis Natural Language Processing Capstone in Applied Data Science II Fall semester: (late-August through mid-December) Introduction to Human-Centered Data Science Python Programming for Data Science Database Systems and Data Preparation Applied Machine Learning & AI The Tutor role will include\nthe following requirements: Support your Faculty Instructor\nin providing feedback and grades for student work Be available via email communication with your Faculty Instructor, with\nresponses expected within 1 business day Familiarize yourself with all course materials on the course Canvas website Attend all course synchronous sessions live. If live attendance is not\npossible, you must notify the Faculty Instructor ahead of time and watch the\nrecordings promptly. Act professionally in all communications with students and with your Faculty\nInstructor Respond to student questions via email (or another designated platform like\nSlack, depending on the course) within 1 business day Alert your Faculty Instructor of any suspicions of academic dishonesty by\nstudents Keep track of your hours worked and share these logs with your Faculty\nInstructor In consultation with your Faculty Instructor, your Tutor\nrole may also include the following responsibilities: Holding weekly office hours to\nmeet with students (either at a fixed time, by appointment, or both) Participating in weekly online meetings with your Faculty Instructor to\ndiscuss course content, logistics, and student feedback Planning and hosting review sessions for students to reinforce and discuss\ncourse materials Coordinating a mid-semester survey of student feedback to inform the\nremainder of the course Supporting the Faculty Instructor with keeping the course Canvas site\nup-to-date (e.g. fixing mistakes, releasing weekly modules, updating deadlines,\nsending announcements, etc.) Helping manage course logistics, e.g. tracking student attendance and\nparticipation, assignment deadlines, late assignments, student groups for\nassignments, student illnesses, etc. Additional Information: Contact Director of Data Science and Assistant Professor of the Practice Emma\nKlugman emma.klugman@bc.edu to request specific course descriptions, or for any\nquestions about the hiring process. To apply for this position, please submit: ● A resume highlighting your technical skills, previous\nteaching experience (if any), academic background, and any other relevant work\nexperience. ● A cover letter listing which specific courses you believe you are most suited\nfor and why. Notes: This is a non-benefited position, hours and\nshifts may vary. The hourly rate for this temporary position is expected to be\n$24.00/hr, with the exception of PhD students and holders who are eligible for\na higher rate at $33.00/hr. Requirements Strong Python skills are required. Academic background/coursetaking in data science and related fields are\nrequired. Prior teaching experience in data science, computer science, or statistics\nare strongly preferred. Excellent organizational, communication, and time management skills are\nrequired. Work experience in data science or related fields are helpful but not\nrequired. Strong communication skills in written and spoken English are required. Closing Statement Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11316?c=bc": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Career Advising & Programming",
      "description": "Associate Director, Career Advising & Programming | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate Director, Career Advising & Programming Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11316 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Career Services Office at Boston College Law School is seeking a new Assistant/Associate Director to join our dynamic, fast-paced office.  The CSO advises and educates J.D. students and alumni on internships and career options in the private and public sectors for legal or legal-related positions, fellowships, judicial clerkships, and alternative careers. In addition to advising, this role is also part of our advising team that considers strategy and provides educational programming and materials to support professional development and the hiring process for all students. Essential Functions Advising: -Provide individual and group career advising to all levels of law students and alumni in both the private and public sectors including short-term and post-graduate programs and placements. -Edit student application materials and conduct mock interviews as needed. -Aid students in understanding the legal market and in aligning opportunities with potential career paths. -Contribute to the department’s data-driven operations and track, record and report on key metrics and analytics. -Contribute to the success of all Career Services events, programs and projects. Training: -Create, organize, promote, facilitate and lead training programs, including in person and virtual, designed to educate students and alumni about career options, application materials, interview preparation and skills, the job search process and techniques and other related professional/soft skills. -Develop and keep current related training materials and other career resources online in a user friendly manner, easily accessible for students/recent graduates/alumni. -Collaborate with student, alumni and other groups to present programs together to improve the overall substance and experience of the program, and the strength of the office’s relationships. Outreach & Department Operations: -Supports these efforts as needed. Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following titles and full-time equivalent hiring ranges: - Associate Director: $86,300 - $107,850; 3-5 years related professional experience - Assistant Director: $78,100 - $97,650; 1-3 years experience Requirements Requirements: J.D. required from an accredited law school. Additional requirements: -Strong knowledge of entry level legal and legal-related job market. -Proven ability to take initiative, develop and execute new approaches, programs and solutions and multi-task. -High degree of professionalism and customer-service orientation. -Excellent listening and communication skills. -Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment. -Excellent oral and written communication skills; outstanding editing skills. -Demonstrated ability to exercise sound judgment in decision making and problem solving.  Ability to earn trust.  Integrity to handle sensitive and confidential information. -Interest in  learning new technology. -Interest in staying  current with developments in the legal industry and other industries where graduates have placement potential, and in the best practices for career services. -Strong knowledge of best practices, techniques and standards for career counseling, coaching, advising and effective job search techniques preferred (required for Associate Director). Experience Assistant Director: Minimum of two years of legal experience as a practicing attorney required, with experience in professional career counseling, legal recruiting, or legal marketing preferred. Associate Director:  Minimum of two years of legal experience as a practicing attorney required. Minimum of three years of career counseling experience or equivalent work supporting law students. Must have flexibility to work hours outside of normal business hours occasionally to meet office needs. Occasional travel possible. Work is conducted in a professional office setting.  Work is conducted primarily in a stationary position and involves moving, ascending/descending, conveying information, and carrying, lifting items of medium weight and operation of a keyboard.  Vision and hearing requirements. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11315?c=bc": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Associate Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11315 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Hiring\nrange (28 hours / week): $48,000 - $60,000; salary commensurate with relevant\nexperience. Boston\nCollege School of Social Work is looking for a Research Associate who is\ninterested in research, implementation science, mental health, and adolescent\npopulation. The Research Associate (0.8 FTE) will oversee research activities\nin the Greater Boston area, and will report to the PI of the project, Dr.\nCarolina Vélez-Grau. This project is funded by the National Institute of Mental\nHealth and consists of implementing brief Interpersonal Psychotherapy for\nAdolescents (BIPT-A) in community settings, delivered by lay providers to\nexamine the feasibility and acceptability of BIPT-A when task-shifted to youth\ncommunity centers. The proposed study aims to: (1) Adapt BIPT-A for use by lay\nproviders (youth mentors) in community centers to decrease depression symptoms,\nincrease belongingness, and reduce burdensomeness among youth with subthreshold\ndepression (Aim 1). (2) Train youth mentors in the adapted BIPT-A (Aim 2). (3)\nConduct a pilot pragmatic randomized trial to test the feasibility,\nacceptability, and mechanisms of change of BIPT-A task-shifted to youth mentors\n(Aim 3). The Research Associate will collaborate with the community agency partners\nto coordinate research efforts, implementation, and dissemination activities. Some\nactivities may need to be conducted in Spanish; therefore, Spanish proficiency\nis required. Primary Duties and Responsibilities * Coordinate and centralize efforts from different community agencies in Boston. * Maintain critical relationships with partners and monitor study progress. * Qualitative and quantitative data management. * Train research assistants on qualitative data analysis. * Lead qualitative data analysis. * Coordinate weekly team meetings between all partners (including PI). * Contribute to writing manuscripts for publication and conference abstracts to disseminate study findings. * Maintain open and constant communication with research team members, partners, and the PI. Oversee and monitor budget and help forecast expenses Assist with IRB applications, including local ethics committee applications as relevant Supervise Boston College students and/or trainees Assist with other duties as assigned by the PI and community partners Application\nProcess Please\nsubmit the following materials: * Current resume or curriculum vitae. * Cover letter (briefly explaining your interest in this position). * Writing sample. * Contact information for three references. Applications will be accepted and reviewed until the\nposition is filled. Requirements A Master’s degree in Social Work, Public Health, Psychology, Epidemiology, or related fields preferred. 1-2 years of previous research experience requiredMental health research experience preferred. Proficiency in Spanish. Exceptional organizational and time management skills, strong attention to detail. Self-starter with experience taking initiative and working both independently and as part of a team. Excellent coordination and collaboration skills. Excellent communication and administration skills. Excellent analytical and critical skills. Have an enthusiastic, engaged, and positive attitude. Previous experience using qualitative and quantitative software (e.g., SPSS, STATA, Miro). Authorization to work in the U.S. required. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11314?c=bc": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Associate Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11314 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Research Associate: The Connell\nSchool of Nursing at Boston College seeks a detail-oriented, proactive Research\nAssociate to support several research studies in various areas (heart failure,\nneonatal health, and aging). The ideal\ncandidate will demonstrate skills and experience at the intersection of project\nmanagement, study support, and data management and analysis. Program Management and Research Related\nActivities Research project tracking : Assist Principal Investigators (PI) in maintaining study timelines and outputs and deliverables associated with the study timeline; coordinate workflow between the PI and other team members (students, other researchers, other staff, external partners); organize agenda and meetings as needed by the PI. Study participant recruitment and monitoring : Work with each PI to implement and monitor recruitment of study participants; conduct initial eligibility screening for study participants; manage study participant enrollment; schedule participant interviews and/or focus groups; execute informed consent; track participants while in the study. Research Activities : Administer study protocols and participant interviews and focus groups as needed by the PI; work with the PI to submit and maintain IRB materials; Assist with the development and/or refinement of study manuals and materials, such as training manuals, standard operating procedures, and monitoring tools. Data Management : Work with PIs to assist in data collection for the study; work with CSON staff to implement data acquisition tools (Qualtrics and/or REDCAP); work with PI and study team to maintain study databases and implement data integration; assist with data cleaning and quality assurance, including processing and management of physiologic and other specialized research data using study-specific software programs, as applicable. Qualitative Data Analysis : Assist with qualitative data management and analysis (e.g., thematic analysis, content analysis, coding); knowledge of qualitative software tools such as NVivo, Dedoose, or ATLAS.ti is preferred; draft codebooks and synthesize qualitative findings into summary reports. Quantitative Data Analysi s: Analyze study databases with tools such as Excel and Stata; assist with data preparation and analysis of quantitative, clinical, survey, physiologic, and other research data as appropriate to the study. The\nsuccessful applicant will work with a dynamic research team within the CSON. The successful candidate for\nthis position will have previous technical experience conducting rigorous nursing\nand/or public health research, be knowledgeable about health care research, and\nhave demonstrated experience with qualitative and/or quantitative research\nmethods. In addition, prior experience working with novel datasets, working\nacross research disciplines, and working with students would be required. Salary Range: 60,000-$75,000 Competitive salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Master's degree in nursing, public health,\n     epidemiology, or a related field 2-3 years of research experience Other Qualifications: Prior experience in project management Demonstrated ability to conduct collaborative and methodologically\n     rigorous research Experience in finding,\n     processing, and analyzing large datasets Experience conducting\n     interviews and/or focus groups Excellent communication,\n     writing, and presentation skills in English Ability to conduct\n     literature searches and produce reviews Strong commitment to health\n     equity and motivation for independent learning Experience with qualitative\n     software such as NVivo, Dedoose, or ATLAS.ti Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11312?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Financial Management Systems",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Financial Management Systems | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Director, Financial Management Systems Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11312 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Assistant Director, Financial Management Systems is\nprimarily responsible for supporting and enhancing the PeopleSoft Financials\n(FSCM) system and other related finance applications, and has demonstrated\nstrong hands-on experience with technical development and system optimization.\nThe Assistant Director collaborates closely with the Associate Director,\nFinancial Systems, Reporting and Planning on system upgrades, integrations,\nreporting, and production support.  Key responsibilities include working\nwith PeopleTools technologies (PeopleCode, SQR, Application Engine, Integration\nBroker), supporting system interfaces and data integrations, troubleshooting\nissues, and automating processes.  The Assistant Director also helps\ndefine and maintain the rules and procedures that guide how the finance systems\nare used and will research and make recommendations on ways to improve how the\nuniversity manages financial tasks by using technology more effectively. This role partners with Information Technology Services\n(ITS) and Finance teams to deliver technical solutions that improve efficiency,\ndata accuracy, and user experience. The Assistant Director also helps support\nsystem upgrades, testing new features, and system improvements. They help\ntroubleshoot issues, assist users, and support financial reporting efforts.\nThis role is important in connecting technical work with the needs of the\nFinance teams and the larger university. This is an in-office position. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $116,000 to $145,000; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Bachelor's\n     degree in Information Technology, Computer Science, Finance, Accounting,\n     Business, or a related field. 5-7\n     years of professional experience in financial systems, with strong\n     technical expertise in PeopleSoft FSCM or comparable ERP system,\n     preferably in a higher education or public sector environment. Demonstrated\n     experience with PeopleTools technologies (PeopleCode, SQR, Application\n     Engine, Integration Broker, Component Interfaces, Application Packages)\n     and ability to design develop and support technical solutions. Proven\n     ability to manage day-to-day production support as well as support\n     functional and technical initiatives related to system upgrades,\n     configuration changes, and process automation. Working\n     knowledge of PeopleSoft FSCM or comparable ERP modules (e.g., General\n     Ledger, Accounts Payable, Purchasing, Asset Management, Travel &\n     Expense, Grants) and related reporting tools. Experience\n     managing financial system integrations, interfaces, and data\n     reconciliation processes involving third party or internal systems. Ability\n     to support system upgrades, patches, and configuration changes as well as\n     automate and optimize business processes using ERP technical tools. Strong\n     troubleshooting and problem-solving skills, with the ability to translate\n     functional needs into technical requirements. Strong\n     interpersonal, communication, and collaboration skills to engage with\n     Finance and ITS stakeholders at all levels. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11310?c=bc": {
      "title": "Associate Director",
      "description": "Associate Director | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate Director Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11310 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Assistant or Associate Director, Counseling Practicum Experience Lynch School of Education and Human Development The Assistant or Associate Director, Counseling Practicum Experience within the Office of Field Placement and Partnership Outreach provides operational oversight for field-based experiences across the Mental Health Counseling, School Counseling, and School Adjustment Counseling programs at the Lynch School of Education and Human Development. Reporting to the Director, Counseling Practicum Experience, this position ensures the implementation and continuous improvement of high-quality practicum and internship experiences that meet institutional, accreditation, and licensure standards. The Assistant or Associate Director oversees student placement systems, community partnership development, accreditation and licensure compliance activities, and field-based assessment processes. Working collaboratively with faculty, staff, supervisors, and external partners, the Assistant or Associate Director serves as a key resource on experiential learning, professional preparation, and student success while promoting excellence, consistency, and innovation across counseling field experiences. Specific responsibilities include: Lead and manage practicum and internship placement processes for counseling students, ensuring high-quality field experiences that support professional development and licensure requirements. Serve as the primary liaison to schools, clinical agencies, healthcare organizations, and community partners, cultivating and maintaining partnerships that expand and strengthen placement opportunities. Oversee systems and processes related to student placement, assessment, supervision, reporting, and documentation, ensuring data integrity and operational effectiveness. Monitor compliance with accreditation, licensure, and professional standards; support accreditation reviews, program assessment activities, and regulatory reporting requirements. Provide consultation, guidance, and professional development to students, faculty, program supervisors, and site supervisors regarding field education policies, procedures, and best practices. Address complex student, site, and compliance-related issues through proactive problem-solving, risk management, and the development of appropriate support and remediation strategies. Analyze field placement and assessment data to inform decision-making, strengthen program quality, and support continuous improvement initiatives. Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following titles and full-time equivalent hiring ranges: Assistant Director: $67,350 - $84,200; 1-5 years’ experience Associate Director: $74,450 - $93,050; 5+ years related professional experience Requirements Master’s degree in School Counseling, School Adjustment Counseling, Mental Health Counseling, Counselor Education, or a closely related field required. Professional licensure or eligibility for licensure in a discipline aligned with the Lynch School of Education and Human Development counseling programs is preferred. Experience working with licensure, accreditation, compliance processes, and university-level student advising strongly preferred. Demonstrated ability to interpret, implement, and maintain compliance with state, national, professional, and accreditation standards governing clinical and field-based training programs. Strong analytical, assessment, and problem-solving skills, with experience collecting, managing, and utilizing data to support accreditation, compliance, program evaluation, and continuous improvement initiatives. Experience managing complex systems, processes, and technologies, including learning management systems, placement management platforms, data reporting tools, and other technologies that support field placement operations. Proven ability to exercise sound professional judgment, manage risk, and independently resolve student, site, and compliance-related issues in a dynamic and evolving environment. Excellent organizational, project management, communication, and interpersonal skills, with a demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities, provide high-quality service, and build effective collaborative relationships with students, faculty, supervisors, community partners, and external stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to culturally responsive, equity-minded practices and the ability to work effectively with diverse populations. Flexibility to work occasional evenings and weekends and travel to training sites throughout Greater Boston, Metro West, and surrounding regions to support student advising, site visits, meetings, workshops, and other program needs. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11311?c=bc": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Major Giving (Southeast)",
      "description": "Associate Director, Major Giving (Southeast) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate Director, Major Giving (Southeast) Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11311 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As we enter the fourth year of the public phase of Soaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston College, we invite you to join our community. Driven by our mission in ways that advance the university for years to come, priority areas for this $3B comprehensive campaign include Financial Aid, Academics, and Student Life. Based on a fundamental Jesuit tenet of developing the whole person, University Advancement is large enough to benefit from many campus resources, yet small enough to be agile and creative in supporting all associates. With an average tenure of close to 10 years at Boston College, our 250-person centralized organizational structure offers a variety of roles across fundraising, alumni relations, operations, and communications and engagement. We invest in the professional growth of every staff member through individual and team-based programs. Likewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking team members who are as ambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We value a culture of inclusion and belonging while actively promoting team members who contribute to the success of University Advancement. See below the current opportunity to begin (or return to!) your Boston College experience! Job Description The Associate Director, Major Giving, reports to the Senior Associate Director, Regional Major Giving and is responsible for the qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of individuals primarily in the Southeast region of the U.S. capable of making gifts of $250K to $4.9M. Specific metrics will depend on the level of the hire, but the position is responsible for building and managing a portfolio of approximately 110 prospects, conducting 110+ substantive visits and raising at least $1.35M annually. To achieve these goals, the Associate Director develops creative engagement and solicitation strategies in close collaboration with Advancement colleagues, volunteers, and external partners. Key Responsibilities: Donor Engagement & Communications: Writes clear solicitation letters and proposals, builds strong donor relationships to drive giving, supports assigned volunteer committees, and develops productive working relationships with key volunteer leaders. Cross-Functional Collaboration: Collaborates effectively across University Advancement, including: o Major Gift Peers: Fellow regional major gift officers o Fundraising Partners: Athletic Development, Corporate & Foundation Relations, Gift Planning, Leadership & Annual Giving, and School Development o Operational & Unit Partners: Alumni Relations, Prospect Development, and Stewardship Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The salary range for this position is listed according to the levels listed below, with the final salary based on various factors, including the candidate’s qualifications, skills, competencies, experience, and internal equity considerations. Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following titles and full-time equivalent hiring ranges: - Associate Director: $99,900 - $124,850; 1-3 years experience - Associate Director: $110,300 - $137,900; 3-5 years related professional experience Requirements This position requires a Bachelor's degree, and for the first level Associate Director, 1-3 years of fundraising experience with a track record of closing gifts of $50,000 or more, and for the second level Associate Director, 3-5 years of fundraising experience with a track record of closing gifts of $100,000 or more. Experience working within higher education is preferred. Qualifications & Skills: Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to authentically share the mission and Catholic, Jesuit tradition of Boston College. Innovative thinking, a strong understanding of process, and the ability to manage and use resources effectively are expected, along with proficiency in technology, fundraising software, Google Workspace, and Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, PowerPoint). A valid driver’s license, with availability for travel and occasional evening or weekend events. A collaborative, self-motivated team player who is excited to support Boston College's goals and community. Must be a collaborative team player who is enthusiastic, highly motivated and dedicated to the goals of increasing alumni engagement and advancing higher education while having an appreciation for Jesuit Catholic education and the goals and values of Boston College. The office of University Advancement supports the mission of Boston College by promoting a culture of inclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting practices support that commitment to build teams reflecting our community; students, alumni, parents and friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11306?c=bc": {
      "title": "Development Assistant, University Advancement",
      "description": "Development Assistant, University Advancement | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Development Assistant, University Advancement Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11306 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/7/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As we enter the fourth year of the public phase of\nSoaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston College, we invite you to join our\ncommunity. Driven by our mission in ways that advance the university for years\nto come, priority areas for this $3B comprehensive campaign include Financial\nAid, Academics, and Student Life. Based on a fundamental Jesuit tenet of developing the\nwhole person, University Advancement is large enough to benefit from many\ncampus resources, yet small enough to be agile and creative in supporting all\nassociates. With an average tenure of close to 10 years at Boston College, our\n250-person centralized organizational structure offers a variety of roles\nacross fundraising, alumni relations, operations, and communications and\nengagement. We invest in the professional growth of every staff member through\nindividual and team-based programs. Likewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking\nteam members who are as ambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We\nvalue a culture of inclusion and belonging while actively promoting team\nmembers who contribute to the success of University Advancement. See below the current opportunity to begin (or return\nto!) your Boston College experience! The Development Assistant (DA) works\nalongside fundraisers, university colleagues and external constituents to\ndevelop, support and maintain programs and activities consistent with securing\nphilanthropic support for the University. Primary responsibilities include: Supports all aspects of fundraising volunteer management, including maintaining the internal database and volunteer management system Prepares materials for fundraiser prospect visits Makes travel arrangements Completes transmittal forms for pledges and gifts received Files visit reports in the Customer Relationship Management database Drafts gift acknowledgments Requests research and pulling additional donor or prospect information from various systems Done well, these tasks assist\nfundraisers in identifying the most desirable prospects and donors. DAs also\nsupport the work associated with external constituents’ visits to campus,\nmeetings and special events, often interfacing with the Programs and Events\nteam, to maximize event effectiveness within budgetary guidelines. Given the\nextensive independent contact with external constituents including alumni and\nparent volunteers, donors, and university officials, this position requires\nstrong interpersonal and organizational skills with attention to detail and accuracy;\nexcellent oral and written communications skills; and complete discretion\nregarding the handling and storing of confidential data. We are seeking to fill two (2) separate\nroles, each situated in a distinct fundraising unit. Candidates will be\nconsidered for either or both roles based on their interest and background. Units\nare listed alphabetically and represent equal, parallel positions: Athletic Advancement: This unit is focused on managing the qualification, solicitation, and stewarding of individual giving from alumni, parents, and friends for gifts between $100,000 $1,000,000. The DA will interface frequently with members of the Athletics department and University Advancement. This work includes partnering with the Athletics Annual Fund and Athletics Stewardship staff, managing the production of Advancement briefings for the Director of Athletics, and producing fundraising progress reports for the Director of Athletics and other internal constituents. Please note that game day support for all home football games is encouraged but not required. Major Giving: This unit is focused on managing the qualification, solicitation, and stewarding of individual giving from alumni, parents, and friends for gifts between $250,000 and $4,999,000. The DA will provide support to the Associate Vice President of Major Giving, the Northeast Director and two Major Gift fundraisers, and the Sr. Associate Director of Benefactor Events. The DA will also provide administrative support for BC’s Pops on the Heights Scholarship Gala and the Wall Street Tribute Dinner. Application\nNote for Candidates: Please indicate in your cover\nletter whether you wish to be considered for Athletic Advancement, Major\nGiving , or Both. If applying\nfor both, briefly mention how your background prepares you for either role. Applications will be reviewed on a\nrolling basis. The salary range for this position is listed below, with the\nfinal salary based on various factors, including the candidate’s\nqualifications, skills, competencies, experience, and internal equity considerations. This\nposition is eligible for overtime. Full-Time\nEquivalent Hiring Range: $47,900 - $59,850; salary commensurate with relevant\nexperience. Requirements This position requires a high\nschool diploma, bachelor’s degree preferred, and 2-3 years of increasingly\nresponsible administrative experience in a professional setting; strong\ninterpersonal and organizational skills with attention to detail and accuracy;\nexcellent oral and written communications skills including effective phone\ncommunication skills; accurate typing skills; a working knowledge of Windows\nPC, including Microsoft Office, and familiarity with Customer Relationship\nManagement databases; exceptional proofreading; strong attention to detail; a commitment\nto accuracy; and very adept at handling multiple tasks and working well within\ndeadlines.  Also requires good judgment,\ndiscretion in handling confidential materials, ability to work independently\nwithout close supervision, and flexibility to work beyond the regular hours of\nthe workday when necessary. Candidates should be highly\nmotivated to work in a fast-paced environment that will require some night and\nevening work. Must be a team player who is highly\nmotivated and dedicated to the goals of advancing higher education and have an\nappreciation for Jesuit Catholic education and the goals and values of Boston\nCollege. A desire to build a career in advancement is a plus. The Office of University\nAdvancement supports the mission of Boston College by promoting a culture of\ninclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting practices support that\ncommitment to building teams reflecting our community, students, alumni,\nparents and friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11301?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Benefits",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Benefits | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Director, Benefits Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11301 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/6/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Boston College HR is seeking an Assistant Director, Benefits, to play an important operational and strategic role in the management, administration, and oversight of the University’s benefit programs and communications. Interacting with over 3,500 active faculty and staff and supporting benefits for more than 1,000 retirees and spouses, this role serves as a subject-matter expert for health, welfare, leaves of absence, and retirement benefits. The Assistant Director ensures seamless automated benefits processing, oversees vendor billing reconciliations, and delivers benefits counseling to the Boston College community. Key Responsibilities: Plan Administration and Operations: Assist with day-to-day administrative oversight for insured and other benefit plans, including medical, dental, life, vision, disability, and Flexible Spending Accounts (FSA). Process life insurance and related benefit claims for deceased participants. Run diagnostic queries, perform data integrity audits, and process weekly/monthly retirement plan contribution files (TIAA, Fidelity). Reconcile vendor billing with Accounts Payable and the Controller’s Office. Leave Management and Statutory Compliance Assist with administering Massachusetts Paid Family and Medical Leaves (MA PFML), Short-Term Disability (STD), Long-Term Disability (LTD), and other leaves in coordination with third-party vendors. Audit leave arrears and coordinate COBRA and retiree billing with external vendor. Retirement and Retiree Medical Oversight Conduct retirement counseling and orientation meetings covering 401(k), 403(b), and 457(b) plans, Social Security and Medicare integration, and retiree medical plans. Support the Benefits Director in the administration of University retirement plans. Assist with Retiree Medical Savings Account (RMSA) administration and assist in monitoring annual IRS retirement contribution limits. Annually update retiree benefit brochures to reflect cost and other changes. Employee Orientation and Communication Serve as a liaison and advocate between employees, retirees, and insurance carriers to resolve claims and other issues. Assist in updating employee handbook links, HR website content, Open Enrollment materials, and general benefits communications. Conduct New Hire Employee Benefit Orientations as needed. Supervisory and Administrative Support Provide functional supervision, task assignment, and training to non-exempt Benefits Specialists/Assistants (1–2 staff members) and student employees. Review and approve Employee Change Request (ECR) forms and payment vouchers. Assist the Benefits Director with annual budget preparation, FAS 106 actuarial census data collection, and plan design reviews. Schedule: Standard in-person office hours with occasional extra hours required. This is an in-office position. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $76,250 to $95,300; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Education: Bachelor’s degree preferred, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience: 3–5 years of progressively responsible benefits administration experience. Experience in a higher education or non-profit environment is strongly preferred. Technical and Functional Skills Systems Expertise: PeopleSoft HR (specifically the Benefits Administration module). Software Proficiency: Microsoft Excel, Word, and PowerPoint; experience with database tools (e.g., FileMaker Pro) preferred. Analytical Skills: Capabilities for analyzing and interpreting benefits statistics, census data, and payroll/billing reports. Customer Focus: Strong interpersonal, advocacy, and customer service skills to resolve inquiries and benefits-related issues. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11305?c=bc": {
      "title": "First Cook - McElroy",
      "description": "First Cook - McElroy | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) First Cook - McElroy Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11305 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/6/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description First Cook Basic Function and Responsibility: Responsible for supervising, monitoring and preparing food in large quantities at assigned work areas for high volume dining room feeding and for specialized catered functions. Moderately heavy lifting is necessary. Characteristic Duties: Supervise and monitor assigned work of second cooks, cooks helpers, food service workers, and general service workers. Participate in preparation of all food-based items on production menus. Strong desire and ability to pursue Boston College food service goals. Under the direction of the Chef or Kitchen Manager coordinates all kitchen functions of support staff assigned to shift/area. Monitors and participates in the sanitation and safety of work and cooking areas and equipment. Instructs employees in proper processes for effective culinary production. Monitors and utilizes inventory controls. Monitors and participates in the sanitation of all equipment used. Reviews work assignments in progress as delegated to support staff. Responsible to assist in the training and evaluation of all service personnel. Responsible to supervise and to participate in the preparation of food for catered functions. Ensures utilization of production standards via use of prescribed recipes. May be required to receive deliveries on receiving dock. Supervise food production to ensure the efficient delivery of food to all serving stations during meal hours. Prior to service check all foods served for taste, temperature, and appearance for maintenance of desired quality expectation. Assist in planning special food functions or dinners as required. Schedule and supervise necessary production for special functions. May be assigned other duties during slow periods such as semester breaks, holidays, and summer. Performs all other reasonable duties as assigned. Supervision Received: Position reports to the Chef and Production Manager of assigned unit. Supervision Exercised: Assists in the supervision of Second Cooks, Third Cooks, and Food Service Workers assigned to operating shift. Hiring Range: $31.39/hr to $34.87/hr. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Qualifications: High School Diploma or equivalent. Two- or four-year Culinary Arts Degree or certificate preferred. Extensive experience may substitute for education. NRA ServSafe certification required and MRA allergen training. Minimum of three years supervisory experience. Demonstrated knowledge of large-scale food production techniques, as well as premium quality catered preparation. Must be a demonstrated leader capable of coordinating and securing the cooperation of a large number of individuals engaged in diverse activities, all of which must be completed within specified time limits. Have outstanding human relations skills. Ability to work a flexible schedule, including evenings and weekends. Must be able to read and write English and perform basic math calculations. The ability to operate basic kitchen equipment. Demonstrated positive work attitude and work history Work effectively with customers, co-workers, supervisors in a high volume, fast-paced environment. Follow verbal instructions and written recipes. Enjoy the challenge of meeting daily deadlines. Lift moderately heavy objects, up to 50 pounds, on an on-going basis. Work effectively in a fast-paced operation with a diverse staff. Have good interpersonal skills. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11304?c=bc": {
      "title": "Teacher Assistant",
      "description": "Teacher Assistant | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Teacher Assistant Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11304 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/6/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Campus School at Boston College is a private, publicly funded special education day school within the Lynch School of Education and Human Development on the Boston College campus. The Campus School educates students  ages 3-22 with extensive support needs, including complex medical needs. Summary: Responsible for assisting the special education teacher with the management of appropriate educational, therapeutic and health care services for each student in the classroom, as indicated in the Individualized Education Program. Primary Duties and Responsibilities: Responsible for assisting the special education teacher with the implementation of the Individualized Education Program for each student in the classroom. Attend transdisciplinary team meetings or other pertinent meetings relevant to implementing the students’ Individualized Education Program and attend in-service trainings. Responsible for following daily classroom schedules and specific programs for group and individual activities for each student as directed by special education teacher. Assist the special education teacher with organization and maintenance of classroom and therapeutic supplies and equipment. Accompany students to therapy sessions as needed. Communicate effectively with students, staff and administration. Under the direction of the classroom special education teacher and therapists, share in teaching in the classroom setting and the development of curriculum materials. Observe classroom settings covering ages 3-22. Assist students in attending to their personal care needs. Support the mission of the Campus School at Boston College Hiring Range: $23.82/hr to $29.78/hr. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Required Knowledge and Experience: Bachelor’s Degree in education or related field and experience working with students with multiple disabilities preferred. Skills/Abilities/Competencies Excellent Communication Skills Excellent Organizational Skills Excellent Interpersonal Skills Excellent Problem Solving Ability Ability to multitask Ability to work as part of a team Ability to work with individuals from diverse backgrounds Ability to respond effectively in emergency situations Bilingual in English/Spanish or  English/Portuguese is preferred Physical Demands: Assist, lift and transport students who have physical disabilities Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11303?c=bc": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Women's  Center",
      "description": "Associate Director, Women's  Center | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate Director, Women's  Center Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11303 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/6/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Associate Director, Women’s Center Boston College’s Women’s Center is seeking an experienced professional to serve as Associate Director , providing leadership for sexual violence prevention, education, advocacy, and confidential support services for students and members of the Boston College community. The Associate Director provides leadership and oversight for the Sexual Assault Network (SANet), including its 24/7 hotline and in-person CARE Team services. The position provides short-term, trauma-informed, confidential support to students and others seeking assistance following sexual violence or intimate partner violence, including safety planning, options counseling, advocacy, resource navigation, referrals, and appropriate follow-up. The Associate Director also supervises SANet graduate assistants, undergraduate student workers, and volunteer advocates and is responsible for the ongoing development, training, and evaluation of SANet services. The Associate Director develops and implements evidence-informed prevention and education initiatives addressing sexual violence, consent, healthy relationships, bystander intervention, and related topics. The position provides education and training to a variety of campus populations, including student-athletes, student leaders, and residential life staff and student leaders, and collaborates with campus partners to strengthen prevention, response, and referral practices. Working closely with University Counseling Services and other campus partners, the Associate Director helps develop and facilitate survivor-focused support groups and retreat experiences. The position also serves as a resource to faculty, staff, and campus partners regarding appropriate support, referral, and prevention practices related to sexual violence and intimate partner violence, while maintaining appropriate confidentiality and clearly distinguishing confidential support services from Title IX, conduct, and other institutional processes. The Associate Director reports to the Director of the Women’s Center and works collaboratively with the Student Affairs Title IX Office and other University partners. The position requires strong clinical judgment, excellent communication and organizational skills, and the ability to respond effectively and thoughtfully to sensitive and sometimes urgent situations. Each staff member in the Division of Student Affairs is expected to support division and university-wide initiatives, staff events, and serve on committees as needed. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $74,450 to $93,050; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Master’s degree in counseling, social work, or a related field required. Independently licensed clinician or eligible for independent Massachusetts licensure within six months of hire (e.g., LICSW, LMHC) preferred. Supervision toward licensure will be provided if needed. Minimum of two years of experience providing direct services to survivors of sexual violence, crisis counseling, women’s health, or a related area. Demonstrated knowledge and experience related to sexual violence prevention, education, and response, including sexual assault, intimate partner violence, relationship violence, stalking, and related issues. Experience developing and delivering educational or prevention programs preferred. Experience working with undergraduate and graduate students in a higher education setting is preferred. Knowledge of Title IX, Clery, VAWA, FERPA, HIPAA, and other laws, regulations, and professional standards relevant to sexual violence response and prevention. Strong clinical, interpersonal, communication, presentation, and organizational skills, with the ability to respond sensitively to individuals with a range of experiences and responses to trauma. Demonstrated ability to assess needs and risk, provide crisis support, develop safety plans, make appropriate referrals, and exercise sound independent judgment. Experience supervising and developing students, volunteers, or other staff preferred. Ability to establish effective working relationships with students, faculty, staff, University Police, health and counseling professionals, and community partners. Demonstrated ability to maintain sensitive and confidential information with care, discretion, and sound professional judgment. Strong administrative skills, including documentation, recordkeeping, assessment, and program management. This position requires occasional evening and weekend work and participation in an established SANet on-call rotation, including availability outside of regular business hours to respond to urgent situations. The work involves sustained interpersonal interaction and exposure to sensitive and potentially traumatic subject matter. Boston College is committed to creating an environment in which students can learn, grow, and thrive. The successful candidate will bring a student-centered approach, sound professional judgment, strong collaborative skills, and a commitment to supporting students while contributing to the University’s educational and formation mission. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11302?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Sr. Associate Director, Corporate and Foundation Relations",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Sr. Associate Director, Corporate and Foundation Relations | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate/Sr. Associate Director, Corporate and Foundation Relations Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11302 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/6/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As we enter the fourth year of the\npublic phase of Soaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston College, we\ninvite you to join our community. Driven by our mission to advance the\nuniversity for years to come, this $3B comprehensive campaign prioritizes\nFinancial Aid, Academics, and Student Life. Based on a fundamental Jesuit tenet of\ndeveloping the whole person, University Advancement is large enough to benefit\nfrom many campus resources, yet small enough to be agile and creative in\nsupporting all associates. With an average tenure of close to 10 years at\nBoston College, our 250-person centralized organizational structure offers a\nvariety of roles across fundraising, alumni relations, operations, and\ncommunications and engagement.  We invest\nin every staff member's professional growth through individual and team-based\nprograms. Likewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking team members who\nare as ambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We value a culture of inclusion\nand belonging while actively promoting team members who contribute to the\nsuccess of University Advancement. See below the current opportunity to\nbegin (or return to!) your Boston College experience! Reporting\nto the Director, Corporate and Foundation Relations, the\nAssistant/Associate/Senior Associate Director, Corporate and Foundation\nRelations role requires positive, productive, and ongoing interaction with\nfaculty, senior administration, trustee volunteers, and major donor prospects.\nThe CFR team plays a critical role in finding funding for projects and\nspecialized research, often serving as a bridge between researchers and private\nfoundations. Specific\nresponsibilities include: Identification of prospects based on extensive analysis using written and online research resources Participating in the cultivation and stewarding of corporate and foundation donors of all levels Involvement in the direct solicitation of corporations and foundations Developing and drafting major proposals and developing fundraising strategies for senior administration and faculty, and other assignments, as necessary. Applications\nwill be reviewed on a rolling basis. The salary range for this position is\nbased on the listed levels, with the final salary determined by various\nfactors, including the candidate’s qualifications, skills, competencies,\nexperience, and internal equity considerations: Assistant\nDirector, 1-3 years of fundraising or directly related experience: $78,100 -\n$97,650 Associate\nDirector, 3-5 years of fundraising or directly related experience: $90,600 -\n$113,250 Senior\nAssociate Director, 5+ years of fundraising or directly related experience: $110,300\n- $137,900 Requirements This\nposition requires a Bachelor’s degree; a graduate degree is preferred. For the\nAssistant Director level, 1-3 years of professional-level experience in\ncorporate and foundation fundraising; for the Associate Director level, 3-5\nyears; and for the Senior Associate Director level, a minimum of 5 years. Preference\nfor experience at a complex institution of higher education or a hospital at\neach level. A combination of corporate, foundation, or organizational\nfundraising and other directly transferable experience from fields such as\ngovernment grant-seeking or corporate philanthropy will be considered for all\nlevels. To\nbe eligible for consideration for the Senior Associate Director, a candidate\nmust demonstrate leadership, self-direction, initiative, and problem-solving\nability and successfully manage a major corporate and/or foundation prospect\nportfolio. The\nposition requires excellent interpersonal skills; a demonstrated ability to\ncompose strong, clearly written material, including general correspondence and\nmajor proposals that articulate funding priorities.  In addition, the successful candidate will\nhave a demonstrated ability to speak and write persuasively on higher\neducation; a working knowledge of research resources for corporate and\nfoundation fundraising; and familiarity with Windows PCs, the Microsoft Office\nsuite, and the Google suite. Must\nbe a collaborative team player who is enthusiastic, highly motivated, and\ndedicated to the goals of advancing higher education, while having an\nappreciation for Jesuit Catholic education, and the goals and values of Boston\nCollege. The\nOffice of University Advancement supports the mission of Boston College by\npromoting a culture of inclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting\npractices support that commitment to build teams reflecting our community: students,\nalumni, parents, and friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11300?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Director, Alumni Class & School Engagement",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Director, Alumni Class & School Engagement | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate Director, Alumni Class & School Engagement Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11300 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/5/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As we enter the fourth year of the public phase of Soaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston College, we invite you to join our community. Driven by our mission in ways that advance the university for years to come, priority areas for this $3B comprehensive campaign include Financial Aid, Academics, and Student Life. Based on a fundamental Jesuit tenet of developing the whole person, University Advancement is large enough to benefit from many campus resources, yet small enough to be agile and creative in supporting all associates. With an average tenure of close to 10 years at Boston College, our 250-person centralized organizational structure offers a variety of roles across fundraising, alumni relations, operations, and communications and engagement. We invest in the professional growth of every staff member through individual and team-based programs. Likewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking team members who are as ambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We value a culture of inclusion and belonging while actively promoting team members who contribute to the success of University Advancement. See below the current opportunity to begin (or return to!) your Boston College experience! Job Description The Assistant/Associate Director, Alumni Class & School Engagement reports to the Senior Associate Director, Alumni Class & School Engagement and is responsible for the planning, coordination and implementation of student, alumni and school-based engagement programming with the primary focus being on GOLD (Graduates of the Last Decade) class engagement, co-leading the GOLD Leadership Council and GOLD Reunion Committees.  In partnership with the Senior Associate Director, the incumbent will be responsible for creating an annual GOLD alumni engagement strategy that involves all members of the Alumni Relations team and their respective alumni groups in an effort to increase GOLD alumni engagement through measurable goals each year. The GOLD and young alumni constituency is a major area of focus for the Soaring Higher campaign and, therefore, this position will be required to help enhance and increase engagement that leads to more meaningful, life-long relationships between our alumni and the University for generations to come. Specific responsibilities include managing the 5th, 10th, and 15th Reunion Engagement Committees, managing the Student Alumni Association, co-leading the GOLD Leadership Council in partnership with the Annual Giving Programs team, and leading First-gen student & alumni programming. In addition, responsibilities include the recruitment, cultivation and training of student and alumni volunteers to work as representatives of the classes, groups and councils they belong to; working with class committees in planning and implementation of individual class activities, programs and events in different markets where a high concentration of GOLD/young alumni currently reside; acting as liaison between classes and university constituencies in the development of each class event; coordinating with the Advancement Communications & Marketing team on ways to best promote and reach these constituencies through tailored GOLD marketing. This person will work closely with Student Affairs and other appropriate departments to provide connections between students and Alumni Relations; will partner with the Annual Giving team to co-lead the GOLD Leadership Council as well as the Councils programming and fundraising goals; establishes policies and procedures for all volunteers to follow when planning annual events; develops operating plans and budgets for programming and events that will achieve objectives and are cost-effective; and establishes and adheres to event standards and timelines necessary to plan and deliver successful programs and events. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The salary range for this position is listed below, with the final salary based on various factors, including the candidate’s qualifications, skills, competencies, experience, and internal equity considerations. Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following titles and full-time equivalent hiring ranges: - Assistant Director: $61,050 - $76,300; 1-3 years experience - Assistant Director: $67,350 - $84,200; 3-5 years related professional experience - Associate Director: $74,450 - $93,050; 5+ years related professional experience Requirements This position requires a Bachelors' degree and for the Assistant Director, a minimum of one to three years professional level alumni relations experience or a combination of alumni relations and directly related work experience, such as events planning or development; the Associate Director requires a minimum of three to five years’ experience working in advancement either in or outside of higher education, alumni relations programs at the professional level, preferable in an institution of higher education engaged in a capital campaign. In addition, this position requires exceptional interpersonal, customer service, oral and written communication skills and the ability to provide the detailed planning, engagement strategy and budget management necessary to ensure that events and programs are cost-effective. Proficiency with Google Drive and Microsoft Office, Excel, and internal database platforms is expected. The successful candidate will have the ability to work exceptionally well with volunteers and know how to build and foster long-term relationships with key constituents and internal University Advancement partners through project based work and co-leading efforts. The incumbent will also need to be able to successfully work in a fast-paced environment, while managing multiple deadlines. Some travel and significant evening and weekend work is required. Must be a collaborative team player who is enthusiastic, highly motivated and dedicated to the goals of increasing alumni engagement and advancing higher education while having an appreciation for Jesuit Catholic education and the goals and values of Boston College. The office of University Advancement supports the mission of Boston College by promoting a culture of inclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting practices support that commitment to build teams reflecting our community; students, alumni, parents and friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11298?c=bc": {
      "title": "Fiscal & Procurement Assistant",
      "description": "Fiscal & Procurement Assistant | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Fiscal & Procurement Assistant Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11298 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/5/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Fiscal & Procurement Assistant The Fiscal & Procurement Assistant serves a key role in the fiscal, procurement, and student organization-related activities of the Student Affairs Business Service Center (SABSC). This position is responsible for the work associated with financial and business operations, including managing and monitoring the accurate and timely processing of all transactions. This role is responsible for responding to incoming requests and inquiries and ensuring follow-up and resolution. The Fiscal and Procurement  Assistant provides financial support and services to Student Affairs departments, and to students involved with the Undergraduate Government of Boston College (UGBC), the Student Organization Funding Committee (SOFC), and registered student organizations (RSOs), including transactional support, reporting, and problem-solving across a wide variety of service center operations and services. Interested candidates must have a strong customer service orientation, the ability to thrive in a fast-paced, team-based environment, and sound judgment to manage complex fiscal and operational processes with accuracy and integrity. The ideal candidate will bring demonstrated experience in financial administration, and problem-solving, along with the flexibility to adapt to evolving needs and contribute to continuous improvement. Each staff member in the Division of Student Affairs is expected to support division and university-wide initiatives, staff events, and serve on committees as needed. Hiring Range: $24.78/hr to $30.96/hr. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Bachelor’s degree Three years relevant experience or equivalent combination of education and experience Understanding of or demonstrated aptitude for budgetary and financial concepts essential, especially in higher education Previous experience in a customer service environment essential Academic experience preferred Excellent organizational, prioritization, and project management skills Functional use of PeopleSoft HR and Financial systems Functional use of Microsoft software including Word, Excel, and PowerPoint Strong oral and written communications skills Excellent organizational, prioritization, and project management skills Ability to recognize and effectively balance the differing needs of students and staff in multiple departments Ability to work well in a fast-paced, deadline-driven environment Ability to work independently and in a team-based environment Availability for occasional evening and weekend work to support business cycles, events, and divisional priorities Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11297?c=bc": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant, Mathematics Department",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant, Mathematics Department | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Administrative Assistant, Mathematics Department Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11297 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/2/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Position Details: Department : 053361 - Mathematics Position : 00011195 - Administrative Assistant Employment Type : Onsite | Full-Time (35 hours per week) | Nonexempt Hiring Range : $28.21/hr to $35.27/hr. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. The Department Administrator is responsible for the overall administrative operations of the Mathematics Department and serves as a key resource to the Department Chair, faculty and students. The Administrator manages a broad range of academic, financial, personnel and programmatic responsibilities, supporting both the undergraduate and graduate programs.  Working closely with the Department Chair and Assistant Chairs, the Department Administrator ensures the effective day-to-day operation of the department while supporting longer-term academic planning and departmental priorities.  The position requires a high degree of independent judgment, organization, discretion and the ability to manage multiple complex processes and deadlines simultaneously. Description of duties: Facilitate the administration of the department’s academic programs, including the undergraduate Core and mathematics major programs and the Mathematics Ph.D. program, in collaboration with faculty. Ensure administrative continuity and support departmental goals and initiatives related to teaching and research. Conduct research and analyze data related to courses, teaching evaluations, enrollment trends and other departmental needs.  Manage departmental budgets and financial transactions, including monitoring and reconciling accounts. Oversee day-to-day office operations and supervise student employees, including hiring, scheduling, training and payroll.  Manage departmental communications, including the department website. Manage expense reimbursements for departmental personnel and visitors, arrange hotel accommodations for visitors and manage logistics for departmental events. Requirements High school diploma plus one year of experience in an administrative role required, preferably in an academic setting. Exceptional written and communication skills and strong problem-solving skills, as well as ability to work both independently and in a team environment. Experience with databases, and electronic payroll and budget processes.  Microsoft Office skills, particularly Excel, familiarity with databases and website maintenance. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11284?c=bc": {
      "title": "Procurement and Fiscal Specialist (Temporary)",
      "description": "Procurement and Fiscal Specialist (Temporary) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Procurement and Fiscal Specialist (Temporary) Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11284 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/2/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Job title: Procurement and Fiscal Specialist (Temporary) Department: Student Affairs Business Service Center Employment Type : Onsite | Nonexempt | Temporary | Part-Time (29 hours per week) Hourly Range: $25-28/hour, commensurate with relevant experience. The Procurement and Fiscal Specialist serves as a key member of the Student Affairs Business Service Center (SABSC), providing comprehensive procurement, fiscal, and administrative support to departments and student organizations across the Division of Student Affairs. This position contributes to the mission of the University by ensuring financial accuracy, compliance with university policies and procedures, and the effective stewardship of financial resources. The Procurement and Fiscal Specialist is responsible for the accurate and timely processing of financial and procurement transactions, including purchase orders, vendor payments, employee and student reimbursements, deposits, budget transfers, and other fiscal activities. Utilizing PeopleSoft Financial and Human Resources systems, the incumbent supports the day-to-day financial operations of the Division while ensuring compliance with procurement, accounting, and financial management standards. This role serves as a resource to Student Affairs departments by providing guidance on procurement and financial processes, responding to customer inquiries, and resolving payment, purchasing, and budget-related issues. Responsibilities include processing and monitoring purchase orders, vendor payments, reimbursements, deposits, and other financial transactions; performing account reconciliations; maintaining financial records; supporting budget management activities; and assisting with fiscal year-end processes. The position also assists with student hiring processes and may provide training and guidance to student employees. Requirements Minimum Qualifications Three (3) or more years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience supporting procurement, accounts payable, finance, business operations, or related administrative functions. Experience processing financial transactions, including purchase orders, vendor payments, reimbursements, deposits, and budget-related transactions. Experience performing account reconciliations and maintaining accurate financial records. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, Google Workspace, Canva and/or Adobe Creative Cloud applications. Strong customer service, organizational, communication, and problem-solving skills. Ability to prioritize competing deadlines and manage multiple projects in a fast-paced environment. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively within a team environment. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively and build collaborative relationships with individuals from diverse backgrounds and experiences. Preferred Qualifications Experience using PeopleSoft Financial and Human Resources systems. Experience supporting procurement, accounts payable, budget management, or financial operations, preferably in a higher education environment. Knowledge of financial controls, procurement processes, and accounts payable practices. Experience creating and managing electronic forms, workflow tracking tools, and reports using Google Workspaceand/or Microsoft Office applications. Experience training staff and student employees on financial and procurement processes. Leadership, supervisory, or project coordination experience preferred. Experience working in customer-focused environments and building effective partnerships with diverse stakeholders. Closing Statement Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11283?c=bc": {
      "title": "Program Nurse",
      "description": "Program Nurse | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Program Nurse Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11283 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/2/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Campus School at Boston College is a private, publicly funded special education day school within the Lynch School of Education and Human Development on the Boston College campus. The Campus School educates students  ages 3-22 with extensive support needs, including complex medical needs. Summary HIRING RANGE: $78,100 - $97,650 Responsible for coordination and monitoring of the total health care program for students with multiple disabilities, including complex health care needs. PRIMARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (List key areas of responsibility, major job duties and key objectives for this position) ● Responsible for daily administration of medications to Campus School students, including obtaining appropriate medical orders from parents/guardians and physicians and ensuring that appropriate dosages and pharmacy bottles are stored in a locked medical area. ● Responsible for recognition, knowledge, and treatment of specific seizure disorders, lacerations, acute illness, nausea/vomiting, human bites, anaphylaxis, chest physical therapy, suctioning, nebulizer treatments, gastrostomy feedings, Mickey button maintenance, and other medical needs or emergencies. ● Provides health education and training to teachers and support staff to ensure that medical protocols are in place for each student in the event of an emergency. This includes training related to food allergies, seizure control, specific disabilities, adverse medication side effects, G-tube feedings and replacements, infection control, unusual precautions, exposure to bloodborne pathogens according to OSHA standards, safety, first aid, and CPR. ●  Communicates effectively and professionally with students’ families and caregivers regarding student health needs, medical concerns, care plans, and follow-up as appropriate. Builds collaborative relationships with families to support continuity of care between home and school. ● Communicates with the administration, in collaboration with the head nurse, in real time on all important matters to address both emergency and non-emergency situations. ●Maintains the confidentiality and security of student health and medical information and follows all applicable HIPAA requirements, school policies, and other regulations governing the privacy and protection of student health information. ● Maintains current professional licensure and completes all required continuing education units and professional development activities necessary to maintain licensure and remain current in nursing practices, regulations, and standards of care. ● Responsible for ensuring that a yearly physical examination is conducted by each student’s physician, including vision, hearing, and dental screening. ● Responsible for management of all necessary immunization information as required by the Department of Public Health and Department of Education, as well as ensuring completion and updating of Intake Packets for each student. ● Completes health care assessments and attends Individualized Education Program (IEP) meetings and team meetings as requested. ● Implements and updates medical policies and procedures. ●Supervises University nursing students while on their assigned clinical placements. ● Works in collaboration with the nurse practitioner consultant in developing health care procedures and supporting student medical care. ●Completes daily attendance records and follows up on student absences as appropriate. ● Supports the mission of the Boston College Campus School. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES Supervises nursing student interns from the School of Nursing at Boston College. Requirements REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE AND EXPERIENCE Minimum Education Requirements Bachelor’s degree; Registered Nurse; DESE School Nurse license preferred (not required) ● BLS ● Welle certified ● First Aid Minimum Experience Requirements Minimum of one year experience working in pediatric nursing with students with special health care needs Skills/Abilities/Competencies Excellent Communication Skills Excellent Organizational Skills Excellent Interpersonal Skills Knowledge of medications and syndromes relevant to population Knowledge of mandated healthcare policies and procedures in special education programs Ability to respond calmly and effectively in emergency situations PHYSICAL DEMANDS Ability to assist/or lift students who have physical disabilities. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11288?c=bc": {
      "title": "Producer, Broadcast Operations",
      "description": "Producer, Broadcast Operations | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Producer, Broadcast Operations Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11288 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/2/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Producer will be responsible for creating and developing\ncontent (including scripts) for live ACC Network sporting events, Coaches\nShows, special segments, special interest stories and other athletic department\nprogramming used to promote BC athletics. The position will assist with other\nimportant aspects of Boston College’s ACC Network broadcasts.  This\nincludes production planning, production and collaborating with External\nAffairs, Athletic Communications and Interactive Media on the dissemination of\ncontent post production. This position is responsible for identifying and selecting\n(under the direction of the Executive Producer) professional game day personnel\nthat will produce all live broadcasts.  These staff members also include\nplay by play announcers and color commentators.  This responsibility also\nincludes staying within a game day production budget given by the Assoc.\nAthletic Director- Technology and Broadcast Services.  The Producer will\nalso be asked to train production staff and supervise the progress of a live\nbroadcast or taped project from pre-production to post-production. In addition, the position will also be responsible for using\ncreative production techniques to enhance all network content.  He/She\nmust be able to collaborate with the network Director, other producers,\nengineers and other key participants about production details and to help\nfacilitate the creation of the “run of show” for live broadcasts and all\nmultimedia content.  Additionally, the Producer must be able to operate\n(and instruct others) a camera and be able to record and edit video and sound\nprojects, including selecting program format for final output,\ncapturing/importing media, organizing raw media, editing footage and sound\nfiles, creating and inserting static and motion graphics, titling, adding\nmusic/voice-over/sfx, media management and archiving project media.  The\nProducer must also be knowledgeable of the changing landscape of live\nbroadcasts with respect to what’s new and accepted in the industry. Flexible daily work schedule. Evening and weekend schedule\nrelates directly to athletic events and other university event schedules. This\nposition includes working on holidays. This position works in multiple types of environments –\noffice, production room, as well as outdoors acquiring video footage/content.\nIn addition, this position is responsible for lifting video equipment which may\nbe as heavy as 45-50 lbs. Reporting Relationship Executive Producer, Broadcast Operations and Production Position Details: Department: 011001 - Athletics General Position: 00014068 - Technical Producer- Video and Audio- Athletics Grade: 3RD Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following titles and full-time equivalent hiring ranges: - Producer: $82,100 - $102,650; 5+ years related professional experience - Associate Producer: $74,450 - $93,050; 1-5 years experience Requirements Bachelor's degree required Master's preferred Formal Experience/Education in Athletic Management is a plus Certified in Crestron Control Systems is a plus 5+ years of experience in the broadcasting industry 4+ years of producing/directing linear and digital\nbroadcasts 3+ years experience in video control room environment 3+ years experience in script contributing or writing. Evening and weekend work is mandatory.  These demands are primarily centered on athletic competitions. Evenings    30% Weekends   30% This position works in multiple types of environments –\noffice, production room, as well as outdoors acquiring video footage/content.\nIn addition, this position is responsible for lifting video equipment which may\nbe as heavy as 45-50 lbs. This position is required to perform all duties and responsibilities in compliance with all NCAA, ACC and institutional rules and regulations. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11282?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Director, Athletics Leadership Giving",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Director, Athletics Leadership Giving | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate Director, Athletics Leadership Giving Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11282 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/1/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As we enter the fourth year of the public phase of Soaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston College, we invite you to join our community. Driven by our mission in ways that advance the university for years to come, priority areas for this $3B comprehensive campaign include Financial Aid, Academics, and Student Life. Based on a fundamental Jesuit tenet of developing the whole person, University Advancement is large enough to benefit from many campus resources, yet small enough to be agile and creative in supporting all associates. With an average tenure of close to 10 years at Boston College, our 250-person centralized organizational structure offers a variety of roles across fundraising, alumni relations, operations, and communications and engagement. We invest in the professional growth of every staff member through individual and team-based programs. Likewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking team members who are as ambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We value a culture of inclusion and belonging while actively promoting team members who contribute to the success of University Advancement. See below for the current opportunity to begin (or return to!) your Boston College experience! Job Description: The Assistant/Associate Director, Athletics Leadership Giving reports to the Senior Associate Director, Athletics Major & Leadership Giving and is responsible for the solicitation of Boston College alumni and parents capable of annual leadership gifts greater than $10K up to $249K. Primary focus will be closing current-use gifts at the $25K through $249K level. The Assistant/Associate Director manages a portfolio of approximately 150 athletics donors and prospects and is expected to meet specific goals for contacts and completed solicitations. Leadership Gift Officers on the Athletic Development team are responsible for a minimum of 130 meetings a year and raising (both originating and collaborating on) $800K - $1M in gifts per year. The Assistant/Associate Director writes effective solicitation letters and proposals and works collaboratively with peers in other fundraising areas, including gift planning, athletics development, school development, and organizational giving to maximize fundraising productivity. In addition, the Assistant/Associate Director works with the Senior Associate Director to develop and implement a comprehensive strategy to increase annual cash giving through the solicitation, recognition, and stewardship of annual leadership donors. The Assistant/Associate Director will also serve as a sport-specific fundraising liaison for assigned teams and assist with donor benefit fulfillment, including but not limited to premium seating, parking, and tickets, as well as database management and telephone support for inquiries through the Flynn Fund main line. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The salary range for this position is listed according to the levels listed below, with the final salary based on various factors, including the candidate’s qualifications, skills, competencies, experience, and internal equity considerations. Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following titles and full-time equivalent hiring ranges: - Assistant Director: $80,100 - $100,150; 1-3 years experience - Associate Director: $90,600 - $113,250; 3+ years related professional experience Requirements This position requires a Bachelor’s degree and 1-3 years of professional-level frontline fundraising experience for the Assistant Director level and 3-5 years at the Associate Director level. A proven record of soliciting and closing annual cash gifts of $10,000-$25,000 at the Assistant Director level and $25,000 or more at the Associate Director level, preferably in a higher education environment, is required. Determination of job level and compensation will be commensurate with experience. Frequent travel, evening, and weekend work are required to successfully perform the duties of this position. Additionally, attendance at all home football games (7) and most basketball and hockey (30+) games is necessary, as is attendance at periodic special events. A valid driver’s license is required.  A demonstrated ability to speak and write persuasively about higher education and the Jesuit tradition is expected.  Proficiency with technology and relevant software applications, such as Google Mail (Gmail), MS Word, MS Excel, and PowerPoint, is expected. Must be a team player who is highly motivated and dedicated to the goals of advancing higher education and has an appreciation for Jesuit Catholic education and the goals and values of Boston College The office of University Advancement supports the mission of Boston College by promoting a culture of inclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting practices support that commitment to build teams reflecting our community: students, alumni, parents, and friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11281?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Director, Schools & Programs",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Director, Schools & Programs | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate Director, Schools & Programs Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11281 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/1/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As we enter the fourth year of the public phase of Soaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston College, we invite you to join our community. Driven by our mission in ways that advance the university for years to come, priority areas for this $3B comprehensive campaign include Financial Aid, Academics, and Student Life. Based on a fundamental Jesuit tenet of developing the whole person, University Advancement is large enough to benefit from many campus resources, yet small enough to be agile and creative in supporting all associates. With an average tenure of close to 10 years at Boston College, our 250-person centralized organizational structure offers a variety of roles across fundraising, alumni relations, operations, and communications and engagement. We invest in the professional growth of every staff member through individual and team-based programs. Likewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking team members who are as ambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We value a culture of inclusion and belonging while actively promoting team members who contribute to the success of University Advancement. See below the current opportunity to begin (or return to!) your Boston College experience! Job Description: Reporting to the Director of Development, the Assistant/Associate Director (AD) will serve as a project manager and content expert to advance key philanthropic priorities across the University to increase support and awareness for innovative programs, faculty and student achievements, research, and interdisciplinary initiatives. The AD serves as a vital resource for colleagues across University Advancement (UA), including: Development, Stewardship & Donor Engagement, Advancement Communications & Marketing, Alumni Relations, and the Business Intelligence and Reporting teams. Core Responsibilities: Content Creation & Messaging: Develop compelling verbal and written content for advancement purposes, including proposals, donor acknowledgments, appeals, briefings, meetings, events, and leadership correspondence. Supply content and help coordinate access to relevant university partners, while ensuring consistency and accuracy of messaging across all advancement channels. Fundraiser Education and Program Development: Create high-impact instructional resources and practical learning tools to educate internal advancement staff and strengthen fundraising opportunities. Data Analysis & Collaborative Partnerships: Utilize data analytics and leverage internal partners (Gift Officers, Stewardship, and Prospect Development, among others) to support Directors of Development in advancing targeted gift opportunities, streamlining internal workflows, and optimizing overall donor alignment. Special Projects & Innovative Expertise: Adapt and develop content expertise in new areas or take on special advancement projects as needed. We are seeking to fill two (2) separate roles , each dedicated to fundraising priorities and focus areas. Candidates will be considered for either or both roles based on their interest and background. Portfolios are listed alphabetically and represent equal, parallel positions: Portfolio A: BC School of Law and the Lynch School of Education and Human Development Portfolio B: Connell School of Nursing, Messina College, Pine Manor Institute for Student Success, School of Social Work, and Clough School of Theology and Ministry (Note: Portfolio coverage areas are subject to change over time as university priorities evolve and there is demonstrated growth within the role). Application Note for Candidates: Please indicate in your cover letter whether you wish to be considered for Portfolio A, Portfolio B, or Both.  If applying for both, briefly mention how your background prepares you for either role. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The salary range for this position is listed below, with the final salary based on various factors, including the candidate’s qualifications, skills, competencies, experience, and internal equity considerations. Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following titles and full-time equivalent hiring ranges: -Assistant Director: $67,350 - $84,200 -Associate Director level 1: $74,450 - $93,050 -Associate Director level 2: $80,150 - $100,150 Requirements Qualifications & Experience: A bachelor’s degree or higher is required. Professional experience requirements vary by role tier: 1–3 years for Assistant Director; 3–5 years for Associate Director (Level 1); and 5+ years for Associate Director (Level 2). Background in non-profit, higher education, or social services organizations is preferred. Core Competencies: Success in this role requires intellectual rigor, exceptional written and verbal communication skills, and the adaptability to manage shifting priorities across different departments. Candidates should bring proven proficiency with CRM platforms (e.g., Salesforce, Blackbaud, or similar advancement databases) and the analytical capability to translate data into strategic decisions and prospect opportunities. This position demands strong interpersonal skills to collaborate across university leadership and staff, alongside a track record of - or ability to articulate - meaningful engagement across varied student, alumni, and prospective donor groups.  The ideal candidate is detail-oriented, highly adaptive, and thrives in high-volume, complex environments. Additionally, successful candidates will demonstrate: Strategic Decision-Making and Creative Problem Solving: Exercise sound judgment and serve as a valued thought partner across teams, using a balanced consideration of facts, priorities, and alternatives. Demonstrate the ability to examine challenges and opportunities in new, creative ways while prioritizing and organizing work effectively. Proactivity and Initiative: Make timely decisions within agreed-upon timeframes and take proactive initiative to identify and resolve complex problems; is a self-directed, forward thinker who can anticipate needs before they arise. Institutional Alignment & Organizational Awareness: Demonstrate a deep understanding of the University’s mission, culture, and constituencies. Understand departmental priorities and collaborate seamlessly across leadership and peer teams to align work with broader university goals. Must be a team player who is highly motivated and dedicated to the goals of advancing higher education and have an appreciation for Jesuit Catholic education and the goals and values of Boston College. A desire to build a career in advancement is a plus. The Office of University Advancement supports the mission of Boston College by promoting a culture of inclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting practices support that commitment to building teams reflecting our community, students, alumni, parents and friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11280?c=bc": {
      "title": "Staff or Senior Staff Psychologist  / Staff or Senior Staff Social Worker",
      "description": "Staff or Senior Staff Psychologist  / Staff or Senior Staff Social Worker | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Staff or Senior Staff Psychologist  / Staff or Senior Staff Social Worker Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11280 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/1/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Staff or Senior Staff Psychologist or a Staff or Senior Staff Social Worker (Grant funded position) University Counseling Services of Boston College has an opening for a full time Staff or Senior Staff Psychologist or a Staff or Senior Staff Social Worker. This is a full-time, 12-month position. Benefits are excellent and the salary range is competitive with peer institutions. We are seeking a clinician with demonstrated skills and investment in working with diverse student populations, and in providing effective time limited individual and group therapy as well as emergency psychological care. Staff psychologists may also have the opportunity to provide supervision within an established postdoctoral training program, and Senior Staff may have opportunities over time to provide clinical supervision to junior staff members. University Counseling Services (UCS) is a vibrant and well respected member of the university community. The mission of UCS is to address the mental health needs of the Boston College community, assisting students in the integration of the intellectual, emotional and spiritual dimensions of their lives. We seek to innovate and welcome opportunities for development of new ideas and approaches to the work, to which new staff may well contribute. The staff of UCS provides a wide range of direct clinical services to a diverse group of undergraduate and graduate students. The UCS staff also works with faculty, staff, and administrators in consultation, outreach and numerous collaborative initiatives to improve mental health and wellbeing of the full campus community. University Counseling Services is highly utilized with nearly 2,000 students receiving individual or group services each year. Counseling Services maintains close connections with the academic units of the university as well as with Student Affairs partners. The department enjoys strong working relationships with faculty, staff and administrators, as well as with various student groups. We work to be a welcoming and supportive resource to all students and the broader campus community. UCS is committed to fostering a welcoming and supportive environment through thoughtful and responsive care. With a focus on the whole person, we dedicate resources to ensuring all students feel valued, heard, and supported in their personal and academic journeys. At the institutional level, BC actively recruits a student body with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. UCS strives to provide high quality, person-centered care for all who seek support. Responsibilities include: Depth oriented and skills based individual and group counseling and psychotherapy Emergency coverage and crisis intervention Solution focused same day therapy Clinical consultation to faculty, staff, students, and families Outreach and training Supervision of post-doctoral fellows, practicum students, or junior staff as needed and appropriate Collaboration with departmental psychiatric providers Evening and weekend on-call is periodically required as a back-up to external emergency coverage. In addition, as a professional, flexibility is expected to ensure that all responsibilities and tasks are completed effectively, even if that means occasionally working beyond regular hours. Some of this work may take place on weekends and outside of normal business hours. Each staff member in the Division of Student Affairs is expected to support division and university-wide initiatives, staff events, and serve on committees as needed. Required Application Materials : Please send CV and Cover Letter, including contacts for two professional references. Requirements Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following: Requirements (across all titles): Expertise in providing psychotherapy, crisis intervention, and risk assessment for a large and diverse student population. Experience in collaboration with campus or community partners, such as faculty, staff, and partner healthcare providers. Background in outreach programming, psychoeducation, or training within a university or community setting. Proficiency in using electronic medical records (EMR) for clinical documentation and case management. Familiarity with culturally responsive care and the integration of mission-driven values, such as cura personalis, into mental health practice. Ability to practice independently. Demonstrated skill and investment in working with diverse client populations. Desired Qualifications: Experience providing therapy for disordered eating Experience working with trauma Demonstrated knowledge and experience in understanding the complex clinical needs related to personal identities and experiences of the full range of students. Previous experience working in a University Counseling setting. Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following: Staff Psychologist: $88,360 -$110,450 Completed doctorate in counseling or clinical psychology Licensure as a psychologist in Massachusetts within 3 months of starting Senior Staff Psychologist: $97,440 - $121,800 Completed doctorate in counseling or clinical psychology; licensure as a psychologist in Massachusetts within 3 months of starting Minimum of 5 years of post-licensure experience providing psychotherapy in a university mental health setting or similar setting Experience with clinical supervision of junior staff Staff Social Worker: $74,440 -$93,050 MSW from a CSWE-accredited program or a Board-approved doctoral degree in social work Licensed Independent Clinical Social Worker in Massachusetts preferred, or ability to obtain MA LICSW within 3 months of starting Minimum of 3 years of post-licensure experience providing psychotherapy in a university mental health setting or similar setting Senior Staff Social Worker: $82,120 -$102,650 MSW from a CSWE-accredited program or a Board-approved doctoral degree in social work Licensed Independent Clinical Social Worker in Massachusetts within 3 months of starting Minimum of 5 years of post-licensure experience providing psychotherapy in a university mental health setting or similar setting Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11259?c=bc": {
      "title": "Plumber 2nd Shift Split, Sunday - Thursday, 3:30pm-12am",
      "description": "Plumber 2nd Shift Split, Sunday - Thursday, 3:30pm-12am | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Plumber 2nd Shift Split, Sunday - Thursday, 3:30pm-12am Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11259 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/1/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Essential Personnel Under the direct supervision of the Shop Supervisor, or other designated personnel, performs skilled journeyman level work in the maintenance, repair, and installation of Plumbing systems, following recognized techniques and procedures according to the National and State Plumbing Code. Repairs and replaces damaged plumbing fixtures: sinks, toilets, showers, bathtubs. Opens clogged drains with plunger or snake. Replaces broken and defective piping.  Routinely inspects connections and other system parts for leaks and corrosion. Perform maintenance, repairs, preventative maintenance using a computerized maintenance work order system. Provide written(electronic) records of work completed on a daily basis. Communicate with Supervisors personally or through electronic forms of communication. (email, phone use etc.). This description is not intended to be all-inclusive. Employees may perform other duties as assigned to meet the ongoing needs of the organization. Base Rate: $48.49 per hour Requirements three to five years recent full time experience in the field as a plumber for a licensed contractor or maintenance organization as a Journeyman Plumber. Current valid Commonwealth of Massachusetts Journeyman’s Plumbers License High school graduate, vocational school graduate is preferred Must be able to read and understand plans, blueprints and schematics related to Plumbing systems Demonstrated ability to work independently and from written or verbal instructions Skill in the use of equipment and tools used in the trade Must hold and maintain a valid motor vehicle operator’s license Must be able to communicate orally, read, comprehend, write, reason and analyze and perform calculations Must be able to bend, sit, squat, stand, crawl, walk, climb, push/pull, kneel, handle objects (manual dexterity), reach above shoulder level, and use fine finger movements Must be able to lift over 50 pounds The employee will be subject to the following Environmental Conditions on the job: Exposure to excessive noise Will be around moving machinery Exposure to marked changes in temperature and/or humidity Exposure to dust, fumes, gasses Will drive motorized equipment Will work in confined quarters Preferred: Wastewater License preferred Experience in Lochinvar boilers and sprinkler systems preferred Specialized experience in laboratory piping and special waste systems, include acid resistant drain systems, RO/Dl water systems piping, acid neutralization tank systems, and laboratory gas piping is preferred. Please note: This is a driving position. Individuals will be required to provide information verifying a valid driver’s license, submit a DMV driving record, and comply with University guidelines regarding insurability. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11268?c=bc": {
      "title": "Dishwasher - Newton",
      "description": "Dishwasher - Newton | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Dishwasher - Newton Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11268 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/25/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Utility Worker / Dishwasher Basic Function and Responsibility: Under supervision, performs assigned tasks associated with cleaning, handling, and transporting of dishes, glassware, silver, utensils, pots and pans. Other duties involve sweeping and moping of floors, cleaning of equipment in kitchen and dining rooms. Daily routine involves the use and handling of detergents and cleaning solvents. Heavy lifting is necessary. Characteristic Duties: Performs a variety of tasks associated with the operation of dishwashing and pot washing equipment, including the washing, handling, storing and transporting of dishes, glassware, silver, utensils, pots and pans. Disposes of trash, recyclables and compost from soiled trays, dishes, pots and pans, as well as work areas and dining rooms. Cleaning of carts and crescors, and kitchen equipment such as ovens, broilers, deep fat fryers. Handles all items with a focus on sanitation and safety. Sweeps and mops floors. Performs other related duties as assigned. Supervision Received: Reports to Lead Utility Worker and unit managers on a daily basis. Supervision Exercised: May assist in monitoring student employees. Hiring Range: $24.96/hr to $26.27/hr. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Qualifications: High School diploma preferred. Additional relevant experience may substitute for education. Previous experience is helpful but not necessary. Person can be trained on the job. Need to work effectively in a high volume and fast-paced environment with diverse staff. Must be able to read and write English. The ability to work effectively in a fast-paced operation with a diverse staff. Good interpersonal skills. Must be able to lift moderately heavy objects, up to 50 pounds, on an on-going basis. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
