    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11261?c=bc": {
      "title": "Temporary Part-Time Mental Health Counselor, Sports Medicine",
      "description": "Temporary Part-Time Mental Health Counselor, Sports Medicine | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Temporary Part-Time Mental Health Counselor, Sports Medicine Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11261 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/22/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Temporary Part-Time Mental Health Counselor, Sports Medicine Boston College is seeking a temporary part-time Mental Health Counselor to provide mental health counseling and related clinical support to student-athletes within Sports Medicine, Murray Center for Student Wellness. The counselor will provide individual counseling, consultation, crisis support, and referrals, while collaborating with medical, athletic training, and campus partners to support the holistic well-being and success of student-athletes. This position is expected to work approximately 20 hours per week and may provide clinical consultation and supervision to other counseling or mental health professionals as appropriate. Competitive salary commensurate with relevant experience with an expected range of $37-$51 per hour. Requirements Master’s degree or doctoral degree in Counseling, Social Work, or a related mental health field. Active independent clinical licensure in Massachusetts (e.g., LICSW or LMHC). Demonstrated experience providing mental health counseling to college students; experience working with student-athletes is preferred. Experience addressing a range of student mental health concerns, including anxiety, depression, adjustment, relationships, stress, and crisis situations. Understanding of the unique mental health, performance, academic, and personal demands experienced by collegiate student-athletes, including the intersection of mental health and athletic participation. Ability to collaborate effectively with medical providers, athletic trainers, coaches, campus partners, and other mental health professionals while maintaining appropriate clinical boundaries and confidentiality. Experience providing clinical supervision or consultation preferred. Strong communication, interpersonal, assessment, and crisis-response skills. Closing Statement Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11257?c=bc": {
      "title": "Director, Advocacy Programs",
      "description": "Director, Advocacy Programs | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Director, Advocacy Programs Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11257 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/22/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Boston College Law School seeks a Director of Advocacy Programs to lead and strengthen its comprehensive Advocacy Program within the Center for Experiential Learning. Reporting to the Associate Dean of Experiential Learning, the Director will provide strategic, academic, and operational leadership for a program that supports more than 100 students annually through mock trial, moot court, negotiation, client counseling, and other advocacy experiences. The Director will help ensure rigorous, practice-based learning opportunities that prepare students to become effective, ethical, and practice-ready lawyers while advancing the Law School's national reputation for excellence in advocacy training. The Director will teach and coach students; develop and oversee training and simulation materials; coordinate in-house and external competitions; supervise the Board of Student Advisors and, as needed, work-study students; recruit and support adjunct coaches, judges, mentors, and other volunteers; and collaborate with faculty, staff, alumni, bar associations, and community partners. The Director will also coordinate events and communications, help develop and monitor the program budget, and lead strategic planning for the Advocacy Program, including opportunities for alumni engagement, fundraising, sponsorships, and grants. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $97,450 to $121,800; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Minimum Qualifications - Juris Doctorate from an ABA-accredited program. - At least five years of relevant legal practice in a litigation setting, including judicial clerkship. - Demonstrated experience and excellence in litigation, including oral advocacy. - Demonstrable managerial or supervisory experience. - Demonstrated ability to teach, train, or supervise. - Outstanding oral and written communication skills and excellent listening skills. - High degree of professionalism, organizational and managerial skills, with strong attention to detail. - Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively in a team environment. - Ability to learn, teach, and manage new technologies. - Commitment to diversity and inclusion. - Familiarity with the advocacy communities nationwide. Preferred Qualifications - Experience in higher education with mock trial, moot courts, or experiential learning. The position requires flexibility to work outside normal business hours, including evenings and weekends, to meet competition and program needs, and includes travel. Work is primarily performed in a professional office setting and may require periods of stationary work as well as moving, ascending or descending, carrying or lifting items of medium weight, keyboard use, and travel to locations outside the office. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions of the role. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11254?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Gift Processing",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Gift Processing | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Director, Gift Processing Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11254 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/18/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As\nwe enter the fourth year of the public phase of Soaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston\nCollege,\nwe invite you to join our community. Driven by our mission in ways that advance\nthe university for years to come, priority areas for this $3B comprehensive\ncampaign include Financial Aid, Academics, and Student Life. Based\non a fundamental Jesuit tenet of developing the whole person, University Advancement\nis large enough to benefit from many campus resources, yet small enough to be\nagile and creative in supporting all associates. With an average tenure of\nclose to 10 years at Boston College, our 250-person centralized organizational\nstructure offers a variety of roles across fundraising, alumni relations,\noperations, and communications and engagement. We invest in the professional\ngrowth of every staff member through individual and team-based programs.\nLikewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking team members who are as\nambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We value a culture of inclusion and belonging while actively promoting team members who contribute\nto the success of University Advancement. See below the current opportunity to begin (or return to!) your Boston College experience! The Assistant Director\nreports to and supports the Associate Director, Gift Acceptance, Finance &\nAnalysis. Primary responsibilities include, but are not limited to, assessing\nand managing all processes involving gift and donor data collection, working\nwith gift processors to address and correct any gift processing issues. This\nposition will be responsible for supervision of four full-time staff positions\n(Electronic Gift Processing Specialists). Responsibilities include: Reviewing and overseeing the batching and the preparation of\n     checks for desktop scanning Managing all bank deposits Maintaining and overseeing all development bank accounts, the\n     daily distribution and assignment of wire and ACH donations Functioning as the front-line liaison with banks for remote\n     desktop deposit reporting Reviewing, approving, and assigning all database transmittals for\n     gifts of $10,000 and above Reviewing, approving, and distributing all stock notifications Lending support to the Fund Financial Reporting and processes The Assistant Director\nexercises considerable independent judgment in reviewing and approving\ntransmittals, assigning staff workload, prioritizing daily processing,\nresolving processing exceptions, identifying gift batch and mail discrepancies,\nrecommending process improvements, and escalating complex issues. They make\nindependent operational decisions affecting gift processing timelines, workflow\nmanagement, and service delivery. They regularly resolve complex operational\nissues involving gift documentation, donor intent, banking transactions, pledge\nadministration, stock gifts, and wire transfers. They analyze multiple sources\nof information to determine appropriate resolution while ensuring compliance\nwith university policies and accounting standards. The Assistant Director will\nfoster a working environment that promotes teamwork and collaboration across\nUniversity Advancement, while supporting ongoing professional development,\nlearning, and training within their team. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring\nRange: $74,450 to $93,050; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements This position requires a\nBachelor’s degree, with a Master’s degree preferred. Background experience\nrequires a minimum of 3-5 years fundraising operations and financial capabilities,\npreferably at an institution of higher education focused or engaged in a\ncapital campaign. The Assistant Director is\nexpected to demonstrate independent judgment, problem-solving, and\nprioritization. Common issues include resolving gift processing discrepancies,\nincomplete or conflicting documentation, reconciliation variances, system\nissues, and time-sensitive requests from internal partners. The position evaluates\navailable information, applies University policies and procedures, collaborates\nwith appropriate stakeholders, and determines the best course of action. The\nAssistant Director exercises operational responsibility over the accuracy and\ntimeliness of millions of dollars in annual philanthropic revenue processed\nthrough the University's advancement systems. The role also addresses staffing\nand workflow challenges by prioritizing workloads, reallocating resources,\ncoaching staff, and identifying process improvements to maintain accuracy,\nefficiency, and strong internal controls. Calendar year-end and\nChristmas holiday work week is part of this position. Occasional after-hours\nwork may be needed to assist financial processing or to meet deadlines. This\nposition requires a strong working knowledge of Microsoft Word, Excel,\nPowerPoint Access, Google email/calendar/documents, learn other reporting tools\nas needed. Preference for experience with a CRM such as Blackbaud. Must be a team player who is\nhighly motivated and dedicated to the goals of advancing higher education and\nhave an appreciation for Jesuit Catholic education and the goals and values of\nBoston College. The office of University\nAdvancement supports the mission of Boston College by promoting a culture of\ninclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting practices support that\ncommitment to build teams reflecting our community; students, alumni, parents\nand friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11250?c=bc": {
      "title": "Data Services Librarian",
      "description": "Data Services Librarian | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Data Services Librarian Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11250 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/16/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Boston College Libraries are seeking a Data Services Librarian to join our Digital Scholarship Group (DSG). As a member of the DSG, the Data Services Librarian collaborates on designing and driving initiatives, providing current services, and is a valued voice in DSG strategic planning. This position is highly collaborative within the DSG, throughout the Libraries, particularly in the cohort (which consists of the DSG, instruction team, and subject liaisons), and across campus. The Data Services Librarian is primarily responsible for DSG collaborations and services related to data gathering, manipulation, management, curation, documentation, and skills training. Such activities include consulting on faculty and student data-driven projects; providing data skills instruction and training on data manipulation and management methods, best practices, and tools in person and through curriculum resource development, e.g., module and online resource creation. These responsibilities are heavily front-facing and involve a significant amount of one-on-one and classroom engagement with undergraduate and graduate students. A large component of this position will involve working closely with a fellow Data Services Librarian and the DSG head on endeavors to grow existing data services and find new ways for the BC community to critically engage with data. It is important that the person in this role is comfortable working across disciplines with a range of data types, including economic and business-related data, which is in high demand from our Economics and Carroll School of Management students. It is also important that the person in this position is comfortable with relationship building and public communication, as everyone in the department contributes to outreach. Please include BOTH a cover letter and resume.   Boston College can not offer sponsorship for this position Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $82,100 to $102,650; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements A master’s degree in Library and Information Science or a closely related, data-intensive research field, plus 2-3 years of relevant experience Demonstrated experience in Providing data skills training and classroom instruction (oral, written, and video tutorials, etc.); Providing research support for students and faculty through data consultations and curation, management, cleaning, and mining services; Working with numeric data in academic, scientific, or corporate environments The ability to engage with research across a variety of disciplines, including economics and business, and a range of audiences, from undergraduate and graduate students to faculty and staff Excellent written and oral communication skills Preferred Experience working in academic libraries; Experience working with big data, survey microdata, text data, or similarly complex data; Experience working with library data resources, data subscription platforms (like WRDS), and publicly available data (like FRED) Demonstrated experience in Data visualization and communication; Data cleaning, creation, and analysis using coding languages such as Python or R; Geospatial data and GIS technologies; Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11248?c=bc": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Biology -  Das Lab",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Biology -  Das Lab | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Biology -  Das Lab Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11248 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/16/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Job Title: Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department : Biology, Das Lab Employment Type: Onsite | Temporary | Full-Time (35/hrs per week) | Exempt Salary FTE Range: $67,000 - $76,962 based on experience The Das Lab within the Biology department is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to investigate the fundamentals of\ncell polarization. The Das Lab is interested in understanding cell-cycle-dependent\nregulation of cell polarity using the fission yeast, Schizosaccharomyces\npombe as a model system. Cell shape establishment and maintenance are\ncritical to cell function and survival. The research indicates that in fission\nyeast, the pattern of cell growth and polarity responds to different cell-cycle\nstages and environmental conditions. This project will investigate how cells\nrespond to different cell-cycle stages or environments to establish their\npattern of growth. More details about our research and recent publications are\navailable at website www.daslabpombe.com . For this research, we will use\nyeast genetics, live-cell imaging, protein dynamics, in vitro reconstitution, quantitative\nmicroscopy, and mathematical modeling approaches. Our lab is well equipped with\na dedicated deconvolution microscope and a TIRF microscope. In addition, the Imaging\ncore at Boston College has several state-of-the-art microscopes, such as a Leica\nspinning disc confocal microscope, Zeiss AiryScan, SIM, and the SoRa confocal\nmicroscopes with super-resolution capabilities for live-cell imaging. Other\ncore facilities include an atomic force microscope, bioinformatics server, and nanofabrication,\nmass spectrometry, flowcytometry, NMR and X-ray crystallography facility. Job\nFunctions The following are examples of typical duties, but\nother duties may also be assigned. Development of the research project, including planning and designing experiments, data analysis. Presentation of project results at meetings and seminars. Publication of high-quality research articles. May be responsible for training staff, graduate assistants, or student employees. Requirements A PhD degree in Bioscience or a related field is required. Candidates with experience in protein and/or membrane biochemistry and biophysics are especially encouraged to apply. Familiarity with live-cell microscopy or yeast cell biology is helpful but not essential. Please attach the following\nelectronic documents to the application: Cover letter, resume, and the names,\nemail addresses, and telephone numbers of at least three references. Please\nreach out to Dr. Maitreyi Das for additional information, Maitreyi.Das@bc.edu. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11243?c=bc": {
      "title": "Bartender",
      "description": "Bartender | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Bartender Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11243 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/16/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Work as a member of a service team to provide both cocktail beverage and meal table service for dining services bar services and catered events. Characteristic Duties: Perform functions of a catering wait staff and bartender for service of cocktails and meals to catered events (i.e. set tables, make cocktails, serve clients, side work, post event breakdown and clean up, and resetting of dining area.) Must be familiar with and food and beverage bar services. Maintain and monitor equipment inventory (china, flatware, glassware, utensils, etc.) and recommend replacement or repair as needed. Perform cleaning and sanitation functions. Able to work on call- flexible and ever-changing event schedule, including evenings and weekends. Demonstrate knowledge of superb customer service and etiquette. Able to continually learn and pass on service knowledge to other team members. Must be Serv Safe alcohol certified for bartending. Proficient at cocktail production with accuracy but have minimal use of written recipes in public. Performs other related duties as assigned. Rate: $24.50 per hour Requirements Qualifications: Education:   High school diploma required. Must understand all aspects of food and beverages bartending service. Serv Safe alcohol certification required. Skills: Must be fluent in speaking and reading English Must have excellent human relation and interpersonal skills. Must have very good organizational skills and able to work in fast paced environment. Must be able to lift moderately heavy objects, up to 50 pounds on an on going basis. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
