    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11241?c=bc": {
      "title": "Senior Associate Director, Content Strategy",
      "description": "Senior Associate Director, Content Strategy | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Senior Associate Director, Content Strategy Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11241 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/16/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As\nwe enter the fourth year of the public phase of Soaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston\nCollege,\nwe invite you to join our community. Driven by our mission in ways that advance\nthe university for years to come, priority areas for this $3B comprehensive\ncampaign include Financial Aid, Academics, and Student Life. Based\non a fundamental Jesuit tenet of developing the whole person, University\nAdvancement is large enough to benefit from many campus resources, yet small\nenough to be agile and creative in supporting all associates. With an average\ntenure of close to 10 years at Boston College, our 250-person centralized\norganizational structure offers a variety of roles across fundraising, alumni\nrelations, operations, and communications and engagement. We invest in the\nprofessional growth of every staff member through individual and team-based\nprograms. Likewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking team members who\nare as ambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We value a culture of inclusion and belonging while actively promoting team\nmembers who contribute to the success of University Advancement. See\nbelow the current opportunity to begin (or return to!) your Boston College\nexperience! The senior\nassociate director of content strategy is a key leader within Advancement\nCommunications and Marketing (ACM), responsible for shaping and delivering a\ncohesive, audience-centered editorial strategy that advances Boston College’s\nfundraising and engagement priorities. Reporting to the director of advancement\ncommunications and marketing, this individual oversees a team of writers and editors\nand ensures that all content reflects the highest standards of quality,\nclarity, and brand equity. A highly skilled writer and\neditor, the senior associate director maintains a personal portfolio of\nhigh‑impact assignments, ranging from donor proposals and institutional\npublications to digital storytelling and multimedia. They set and uphold\neditorial standards across the team, ensuring that every piece of content is\npolished, compelling, and aligned with BC’s institutional voice. This role also\ncollaborates closely with colleagues across ACM, Development, Alumni Relations,\nand University Communications to source narratives and deliver cohesive,\ncross‑functional work. Primary Duties: Lead editorial team: Manage and develop a team of writers and editors, including project assignment, reviewing work, performance feedback, coaching growth, and ensuring adherence to deadlines as well as consistent quality and messaging across all content Shape messaging frameworks: Contribute to the creation of messaging frameworks for University Advancement priorities Oversee content development: Lead concepting and copy development for both broad engagement touchpoints and custom, high-impact materials including donor solicitations, publications, and multimedia assets Drive strategic content planning: Develop strategies to maximize existing communication platforms and publications, ensuring alignment with institutional priorities and audience needs Champion audience-centered work: Ensure content is grounded in audience insight and segmentation to deliver relevant, engaging communications that build long-term trust Apply institutional voice: Uphold BC’s style, voice, and brand standards across all segments, constituencies, and publications Source and shape narratives: Partner with University Advancement stakeholders to identify stories that reflect business objectives while meeting editorial needs Maintain editorial excellence: Ensure high standards of copywriting and quality control while managing multiple projects and deadlines Build cross-functional partnerships: Collaborate across ACM and University Advancement to align priorities, share insights, and deliver cohesive, integrated communications Applications\nwill be reviewed on a rolling basis. The salary range for this position is\nlisted below, with the final salary based on various factors, including the\ncandidate’s qualifications, skills, competencies, experience, and internal\nequity considerations. Full-Time\nEquivalent Hiring Range: $104,950 to $131,200 salary commensurate with relevant\nexperience. Requirements Minimum of five years of professional writing experience, preferably in a development-related environment Demonstrated experience managing and developing staff Strong organizational skills, attention to detail, and the ability to prioritize in a fast-paced, feedback-rich environment Innate drive and collaborative spirit Appreciation for mission-driven institutions and commitment to advancing higher education and Boston College’s Jesuit, Catholic values Bachelor’s degree required; advanced degree in communication, English, journalism, or a related field preferred Sense of humor appreciated The office of University\nAdvancement supports the mission of Boston College by promoting a culture of\ninclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting practices support that\ncommitment to build teams reflecting our community; students, alumni, parents\nand friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11240?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Programs & Operations",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Programs & Operations | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Director, Programs & Operations Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11240 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/15/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Lynch Leadership Academy seeks an Assistant Director of\nProgram and Operations who will be provide programmatic leadership and is\nresponsible for the implementation of specific program aspects of Lynch\nLeadership Academy, including but not limited to: recruitment and selection,\ncommunications, and Fellowship programming.  The Assistant Director is responsible for supporting fiscal, human\nresources and operational functions associated with the academy. The Assistant Director communicates regularly with\nUniversity personnel in other departments and is often the primary liaison\nbetween LLA and the University especially with items like event logistics,\nfinance, and communication. Reporting to the Lynch Leadership Academy Director of Finance and Operations responsibilities\ninclude: Lead the outreach, application, interview and selection processes for interested program participants. Examine program processes and\n     protocols for inequities and propose and implement solutions Lead all logistics, so every\n     professional development effort - days, retreats, institutes, school\n     visits - run without a hitch. Contribute to proposal and\n     program design for our Fellowship and consulting partnerships Contribute to our regional\n     and national expansion efforts; help to identify, evolve and launch pilot\n     initiatives. Responsible for all LLA social media, increasing our followers and aligning our social media with our values, strategy and programs Develop policies and establish procedures to maximize effectiveness and efficiency. Support the Director of Finance and Operations in the financial operations of LLA, including paperwork for facilitators, MOUs and invoices, purchasing, reimbursements, and reconciling spending. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $61,050 to $76,300; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Minimum 3-5 years of experience in program management and operations, education and/or non-profit experience preferred Outstanding oral and written communication skills Detail-oriented and tech-savvy. Previous experience supporting financial management preferred Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11234?c=bc": {
      "title": "Second Cook- McElroy",
      "description": "Second Cook- McElroy | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Second Cook- McElroy Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11234 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/10/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Second Cook Basic Function and Responsibility: Under direction, prepares and cooks large quantities of food as assigned for high volume dining room feeding and for specialized catered functions. Moderately heavy lifting is necessary, up to 50 pounds. Characteristic Duties: As assigned, prepares and cooks, from recipes, large quantities of food and menu items. Assists in the organization of the kitchen for the proper preparation of food. Participates in breakfast, lunch, receptions, dinner and/or catered events. Hand carves and/or sautees to order food items at catered events. Demonstrate and practices knowledge of proper health code sanitation and safety standards. Assists, as directed, in the organization and training of cook's helpers, food service workers, and students assigned to work area in the preparation of food and the proper operation of relevant equipment. May assist in the monitoring of the preparation and cooking of food in assigned area. May be assigned to assist in the transportation and delivery of food and equipment for special function catering. Participates, in assuring that food is properly panned and garnished in a timely manner in order to meet the service and quality needs of the operation. May be assigned other duties during slow periods such as semester breaks, holidays and summer. Performs all other related duties as assigned. Supervision Received: Reports to the Chef Manager in assigned unit, may receive functional direction from unit manager on duty, chef or first cook. Supervision Exercised: Assist in the monitoring of work by cook's helpers and food service workers and other support personnel. Hiring Range: $29.17/hr to $32.41/hr. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Qualifications: High School Diploma. Extensive experience may substitute for education. NRA ServSafe certification required and MRA Allergen certification. Minimum of 2 -3 years of quality large volume institutional cooking. Supervisory experience preferred. Must be able to read and write English. Must be able to effectively work in a fast-paced operation with a diverse staff. Must have outstanding human relations skills. Must be able to operate basic kitchen equipment. Must be able to lift moderately heavy objects, up to 50 pounds on an on-going basis. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11233?c=bc": {
      "title": "Third Cook - Corcoran Commons",
      "description": "Third Cook - Corcoran Commons | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Third Cook - Corcoran Commons Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11233 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/10/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Third Cook Basic Function and Responsibility Under direction, performs a variety of routine, supportive duties to assist in the preparation of food for use in the dining operations and/or catered events. Moderately heavy lifting is necessary. Characteristic Duties -As assigned, assists in the preparation of food items using recipes for all meals (breakfast, lunch, receptions, and dinner). -As assigned, provides routine support in kitchen by performing duties such as, slicing, mixing, grilling, sauteing, roasting, steaming, baking and stewing. -Under direction of individual in charge, monitors foods while cooking to maintain proper temperatures and ensure proper timing cycles. -Transports food to serving areas, assembles and replenish food items as required. -May transport food and equipment to catered functions and then assists in the execution of events as assigned. -Performs routine sanitation and safety functions of work areas and equipment. -May assist in the training of food service workers and students as directed. -May be assigned other duties during slow periods such as semester breaks, holidays and summer. -Performs all other reasonable duties as assigned. Supervision Received Reports to the Chef Manager in assigned unit, while receiving functional direction from unit chef, first, cook and other support personnel. Supervision Exercised May assist in the monitoring of work by food service workers and students as required. Hiring Range: $25.78/hr to $28.65/hr. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Qualifications Education: High School diploma. Additional relevant experience may substitute for education. NRA ServSafe certification and MRA allergen training required. Experience: Minimum of 1 year experience in a large volume food operation. Skills: Must be able to read and write English. Must be able to work effectively in a fast-paced operation with a diverse staff. Must have outstanding human relations skills. Must be able to operate basic kitchen equipment. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11230?c=bc": {
      "title": "Executive Director, Krantz Institute for Artificial Intelligence, Ethics, and Humanity",
      "description": "Executive Director, Krantz Institute for Artificial Intelligence, Ethics, and Humanity | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Executive Director, Krantz Institute for Artificial Intelligence, Ethics, and Humanity Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11230 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/9/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description About Boston College Founded in 1863, Boston College is one of the 27 Jesuit, Catholic universities in the United States. It is dedicated to the pursuit of truth and the use of knowledge in service to the common good. The University’s mission is rooted in a world view that values rigorous intellectual development and ethical formation.Institutional Overview & Mission Alignment. Boston College is embarking on a transformative initiative with the establishment of the Krantz Institute for Artificial Intelligence, Ethics, and Humanity . Enabled by a major gift from University Trustee Jason Krantz '95, P'23, and Keely (Fitzgerald) Krantz '95, P'23, the Institute will leverage BC’s formative and ethical strengths as a Jesuit, Catholic university to explore the opportunities, implications, and pitfalls of artificial intelligence. Inspired by Pope Leo’s recently published encyclical, Magnifica Humanitas , which underscores education’s role in an era of rapid technological change, the Institute brings BC’s rich heritage of thoughtful reflection and discernment into a lively engagement with the promise and perils of a particularly transformative technology. The institute aims to inform emerging discussion of AI and to instill in students the judgment, wisdom, and critical thinking necessary to become moral leaders and creators of AI, ensuring that humanistic values and ethics remain foundational to the world's evolving approach to AI. Position Overview & Strategic Direction Reporting to the Provost and Dean of Faculties, the Executive Director will build an Institute that will position Boston College as a national and global leader in teaching, research, and discussion about how to best leverage artificial intelligence for the benefit of humanity, as well as to scale foundational and applied AI initiatives across all nine of the schools and colleges at Boston College. Working closely with faculty colleagues, academic leadership, and external partners, the successful candidate will build this early-stage platform into a sustainable, world-class hub of institutional excellence. The Executive Director will be charged with driving three primary goals: Convene Global Thought Leaders: Organize world-class conferences and symposia at Boston College to explore AI’s potential impact on education and humanity. The Executive Director will partner with academics across and beyond campus to sustain a lively and ongoing conversation about AI, ethics, and humanity. Cultivate Student Formation and Leadership: Offer fellowships, internships, and experiential learning opportunities to equip BC students with the experience and expertise to participate thoughtfully in how the world should engage with AI. Advance Curricular & Internal Innovation: Imagine and implement campus-wide AI programs, support the integration of AI-related topics across disciplines, and award internal seed grants to support innovation in applying AI to teaching, research, and higher education operations. Core Programmatic Pillars The Executive Director will develop, operationalize, and guide the institute's core strategic pillars to catalyze campus engagement with AI and to establish a clear framework for investment and prioritization with an ambition to inform national and global conversations about AI: AI & Human Flourishing: Exploring AI in light of fundamental questions about ethics and human dignity, and drawing on the University’s distinctive strengths in the humanities. AI & Democracy: Examining AI’s various effects on government and public life, in the United States and beyond. AI & the Economy: Guiding organizations on how to responsibly leverage AI and engaging with industry leaders (including partnering with the Carroll School of Management and the 2027 Finance Conference). AI & Health Care: Investigating how AI can improve healthcare outcomes while recognizing emerging threats to human-centered care for the vulnerable. AI & Formative Education: Imagining new possibilities for BC’s distinctive approach to teaching and learning in the context of the AI revolution. AI & Faith: Rooted in BC's Jesuit, Catholic heritage and mission, this pillar is at once the Institute's clearest differentiator and an area where Boston College is called to provide leadership to colleges, schools, and other faith-based organizations. Within each pillar, the Executive Director will develop a robust set of mechanisms appropriate to the theme; examples include research grants, undergraduate fellowships, curriculum integration, industry partnerships, conferences and symposia, and visiting scholars. Key Responsibilities and Scope Strategic & Operational Leadership: Partner with faculty and academic leadership to execute long-term strategies, balancing academic priorities with rigorous operational execution. Interdisciplinary Integration: Connect technical AI researchers with diverse fields, including business, law, education, social work, nursing, and the humanities, to advance an expansive portfolio of creative pedagogy and use-inspired research. Faculty Support: Facilitate large-scale, multi-investigator research proposals and support the recruitment, retention, and mentorship of senior faculty, tenure-track hires, postdoctoral scholars, and students. External Partnerships & Public Voice: Build strong partnerships with non-profit organizations, for-profit companies, government agencies, and policymakers to enable real-world experimentation. Serve as an important public voice on the ethical and social implications of emerging technologies. Fundraising & Advancement: Partner with University Advancement in fundraising and donor engagement efforts to expand the Institute's reach and global impact. Requirements Experience, Qualifications, and Expectations Leadership Experience: At least 10 years of demonstrated leadership experience leading complex organizations, large collaborative initiatives, and/or multidisciplinary research programs. Education: A bachelor’s degree is required. A Ph.D. or the equivalent terminal degree is preferred. Given the institute’s fundamentally interdisciplinary mission, the university welcomes candidates from a broad range of disciplines, including the humanities and social sciences, as well as from computational fields such as computer science, engineering, statistics, and data science. Academic and Professional Credibility: The ideal profile includes an internationally recognized record of scholarly or professional achievement sufficient for a tenured faculty or professor of the practice appointment, along with the proven ability to navigate a mission-driven organizational environment. Core Competencies: Exceptional communication, resource management, and relationship-building skills. An entrepreneurial mindset capable of building institutions from the ground up and bridging academia, civil society, and industry. Mission Commitment: A profound, demonstrable commitment to advancing Boston College’s defining Jesuit and Catholic mission of formative education and ethical discernment. How to Apply Interested candidates should submit a comprehensive CV and a cover letter detailing their interest in the role and their strategic vision for the Krantz Institute. Boston College is an equal opportunity employer committed to fostering a diverse and welcoming environment. The expected salary range for this position is $250,000-$350,000. The actual pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as, but not limited to, candidate qualifications and prior experience, scope of responsibilities, internal equity, and market considerations. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11228?c=bc": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Procurement Services",
      "description": "Associate Director, Procurement Services | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate Director, Procurement Services Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11228 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/8/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Associate Director, Procurement Services , leads, directs,\nand participates in efforts that lead to cost optimization, improved quality,\nand enhanced innovation through the development of strategic sourcing\ninitiatives, effective contracting, negotiation tools, and specification\ndevelopment.  This position will also develop\nstandard processes and expected levels of execution related to the development\nof functional and category strategic sourcing programs, and will oversee the\nexecution of vendor selection. The Associate Director manages the Procurement teams\nresponsible for the strategic sourcing of all commodities and services by\nproviding oversight to team members efforts in market assessments, RFP, bid\nstrategy development, supplier negotiations, and contract development and\nimplementation activities.  In addition\nwill be responsible for strategic cost management requiring extensive\ncross-functional interface within the University community, and will furnish\nprocurement analysis and cost data for planning, budgeting, and other\nfunctional duties. The Associate Director acts in a consultative role and works\nwith departments throughout the University by determining how the Procurement\norganization can assist in achieving their goals by understanding each\ndepartment’s unique purchasing needs and budgets, and developing sourcing plans\nand strategies that best meets those needs.  This position is also the primary conduit for the multiple Consortium\npartnerships of the University and uses his/her knowledge of spend and\ntechnology in order to lower costs for Consortium members and the University. This is an in-office position. To be considered, please apply with a resume and cover letter. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $124,950 to $156,200; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements 7+ years of progressively responsible\nprofessional procurement experience, preferably in a higher education\ninstitution or Fortune 1000 company. A minimum of 3 years of supervisory\nexperience. Bachelor’s degree is\nrequired. An advanced degree in business is preferred. Certification by a recognized professional\norganization is strongly preferred; including: Certified Purchasing Manager\n(CPM), Certified Associate in Contract Management (CACM), Certified\nProfessional Contract Manager (CPCM), or Certified Purchasing Professional\n(CPP) is strongly preferred. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11223?c=bc": {
      "title": "Resident Director",
      "description": "Resident Director | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Resident Director Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11223 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/4/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Resident Director, Residential Life, Student Affairs The mission of the Office of Residential Life is to create a respectful, healthy, and safe living community for our residents. We are committed to integrating the Roman Catholic and Ignatian tradition into the formation of each student by connecting their intellectual, ethical, religious, and spiritual lives through a myriad of programs and services. Our role in this formational development is achieved by promoting the academic mission, modeling and encouraging responsible and ethical behaviors in conformity with the religious and moral principles of the Roman Catholic Church, engaging residents in conversations that foster mutual respect and understanding, and creating safe, well-managed learning environments where students can seek the greater good in conformity with the Jesuit tradition of service to others. Under the leadership of the Associate Vice President and Director of Residential Education and Director of Operations, the department consists of 40 professional staff members, 24 graduate positions, 200 RAs, and additional student employees. Basic Function and Responsibility A Resident Director position is a 12-month, full-time live-in professionals responsible for developing a community of 250 to 900 residents. The Resident Director (RD) lives with, ministers to, and provides support services directly to students. The RD supervises a staff of 8-20 Resident Assistants and Graduate Assistants. They also oversee all operational and programmatic activities of BC’s residences and adjudicate student conduct cases. Summer responsibilities may include participation in department-wide initiatives or special project teams (e.g., Summer Student Housing or Conferences, Training Teams, etc.). By personal example, the Resident Director strives to create a safe and welcoming environment for all students by living, reflecting, and expressing the values of a Jesuit and Catholic institution, as defined by the teachings of the Catholic Church with a special commitment to respect, community, and social justice. Successful candidates will also be administratively strong, culturally competent, possess a Master’s degree, and have prior residence life or related student affairs experience. The Resident Director receives direct supervision from an Assistant Director, and functional supervision from multiple Assistant or Associate Directors, or the Director of Residential Education. Primary Areas of Responsibility Staff Supervision The Resident Director is responsible for the following staff supervision in their assigned community: Models for students and advocates for a commitment to the Jesuit and Catholic values of Boston College, as defined by the teachings of the Catholic Church, with special attention to respect, community,  and social justice Assists in the selection and training of undergraduate and graduate staff Plans and conducts staff meetings on a weekly basis Meets with each staff member individually on a regular basis Plans and implements staff development programs Advises staff on appropriate student and floor interventions Addresses concerning trends or behavior, as well as recognizes positive efforts Student Development/Community Standards The Resident Director is responsible for the following aspects of student development in their assigned residential community: Intentional interactions and conversations Living among students and setting an appropriate standard for residential living Oversees the programming efforts and supervises graduate assistants and RAs in program generation and responsible budgeting Serves as a Conduct Hearing Officer for violations of university policy Responds as Resident Director on Call (RDOC) for crisis situations, including during non-business and weekend hours Provides academic and personal counseling to students, making referrals when appropriate Nurtures and supports relationships with their Campus Ministry representatives (Graduate or Resident Ministers) in the service of expressing and modeling the Jesuit and Catholic values of Boston College Engages resident population on current events including topics of community engagement, social responsibility, and being a productive community member May advise Residence Hall Council (first-year area only) or other student leadership organizations Operations The Resident Director is responsible for the following aspects of operations in their assigned community: Facilitates the opening and closing of their community Conducts health and safety inspections each semester Coordinates follow-up for all maintenance and housekeeping concerns/requests through, and with, the facilities team Conducts regular walk-throughs of community Maintains regular office hours Monday through Friday and will periodically work on nights and weekends for essential responsibilities throughout the year Facilitates check-in/check-out procedures and room changes Works with the Assistant Director of Housing Assignments & Occupancy to ensure proper occupancy records and reports On-Call Responsibilities The Resident Director is responsible for the following aspects of crisis response while on duty: Participates in the university Crisis Response duty rotation. When on duty the RD is expected to respond to crises, both on campus and in the surrounding neighborhood, in accordance with our policies and response guidelines Responds to students and parents on Residential Life issues or concerns Assists with duty during sporting and special events which may be on weekends or in the evening hours Acts as a liaison with Boston College Police Department, University Counseling Services, the Primary Care Center, faculty, and other University offices during emergencies Department and University Relations The Resident Director participates in the following department and university relations: Serves on departmental committees, and may have an opportunity to serve on division or university-wide committees Attends department and area staff meetings, in-service training programs, and one-on-one meetings with their respective supervisor Acts as a liaison with faculty, the Jesuit community, and university offices Summer responsibilities may include Training, Operations, Summer Housing, or Special Projects, as assigned by the Director or Associate Directors Assists with the implementation of university and departmental goals and objectives, and other duties as assigned Each staff member in the Division of Student Affairs is expected to support division and university-wide initiatives, staff events, and serve on committees as needed. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $51,350 to $64,200; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements A Master’s degree in Higher Education, Student Personnel, Counseling, or related field is strongly preferred; Bachelor’s degree required 1-3 years of progressively responsible experience working in a student affairs setting at the professional or graduate level Prior residence hall experience at the graduate or professional level preferred Comprehension and appreciation of the Jesuit and Catholic values and mission and the traditions and heritage of Boston College, and a willingness to express and abide by those values and mission Demonstrated commitment to fostering a welcoming, respectful, and supportive environment for individuals from varied backgrounds and experiences. Demonstrated understanding of student development theory Strong leadership, communication, and interpersonal skills Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11215?c=bc": {
      "title": "Fiscal & Procurement Specialist, Arts & Sciences Service Center",
      "description": "Fiscal & Procurement Specialist, Arts & Sciences Service Center | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Fiscal & Procurement Specialist, Arts & Sciences Service Center Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11215 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/3/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Position : 00012430 - Fiscal & Procurement Specialist Department : 053011 - Arts & Sciences Service Center Employment Type : On-Site | Nonexempt | Full-Time Hiring Range : $24.78/hr to $30.96/hr., 35 hours per week. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. The Fiscal & Procurement Specialist serves a key customer service support role for the fiscal and procurement operations of the Morrissey College of Arts and Sciences. The position works as part of a team that supports 23 departments within the Morrissey College.  The position assists the Service Center team with matters related to procurement, payment processing and compliance while contributing to the Service Center’s effectiveness and successful daily operations. Reporting to the Associate Director, the Fiscal & Procurement Specialist will be responsible for managing procurement operations, processing vouchers and expense reimbursements, reconciling and approving P-card transactions, and assisting with budgeting and compliance activities.  It involves interaction with other departments including Purchasing, Accounts Payable, and  the Office for Sponsored Programs. University operating and grant policies and procedures must be understood and applied. Requirements Three or more years’ relevant experience or equivalent combination of education and experience. Strong organizational skills and ability to prioritize multiple tasks. Ability to work collaboratively in a team-based environment and to work independently. Excellent interpersonal, communication and analytical skills. Experience in a fast-paced, customer service environment. Experience working with procurement and payment processes. Proficiency in Microsoft Office, Excel, Word, and financial software applications. A bachelor degree is preferred. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11213?c=bc": {
      "title": "Temporary Custodian III, Monday-Friday, 11pm-7:30am. 11pm Sunday start",
      "description": "Temporary Custodian III, Monday-Friday, 11pm-7:30am. 11pm Sunday start | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Temporary Custodian III, Monday-Friday, 11pm-7:30am. 11pm Sunday start Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11213 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/3/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Please note: This is a driving position. Individuals will be required to provide information verifying a valid driver’s license, submit a DMV driving record, and comply with University guidelines regarding insurability. Responsibilities include all Housekeeping duties as assigned, such as cleaning, vacuuming, removal of trash, project work, recycling, spot cleaning carpets and brite work. Will also perform major project work including machining and refinishing floors, shampooing carpets and washing walls. Will also clear trash from entrance ways and remove snow during the winter. Responsibilities include all Housekeeping duties as assigned, such as: -Cleaning, vacuuming, removal of trash, project work, recycling, spot cleaning carpets and brite work. -Will also perform major project work including machining and refinishing floors, shampooing carpets and washing walls. -Will also clear trash from entrance ways and remove snow during the winter. Pay - 24.42+ Shift differential Requirements Please note: This is a driving position. Individuals will be required to provide information verifying a valid driver’s license, submit a DMV driving record, and comply with University guidelines regarding insurability. * An ability to perform heavy and repetitive physical labor, lifting up to 50 lbs. * The ability to communicate/comprehend both verbally and in writing. This includes: reading and understanding work schedules, label warnings on cleaning products; writing work orders, taking direction and communicating with staff, students, and faculty. * Must maintain a valid driver's license Closing Statement Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
