    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11188?c=bc": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Graduate Admissions & Financial Aid",
      "description": "Associate Director, Graduate Admissions & Financial Aid | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate Director, Graduate Admissions & Financial Aid Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11188 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/27/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Associate Director, Graduate Admissions & Financial Aid As a critical member of the Graduate Admission and Financial Aid team at the Lynch School of Education and Human Development, the Associate Director will serve in a cross functional role to support the mission of the Graduate Enrollment Office. In its first primary function, the Associate Director will provide leadership and management to the office staff in order to promote a smooth and positive applicant experience. The Associate Director supervises staff and oversees the day-to-day office operations. The Associate Director will be an expert in the Lynch School graduate programs and the admissions process in order to provide quality guidance to prospective students and to the front-line staff. In its second primary function, the role will support strategic enrollment efforts through the administration and management of financial aid and scholarship programs, including managing budgets and advising prospective and current students on financial packages and options. In addition, the Associate Director will be a key contributor to recruitment and yield events, serving as lead on some events and presenting at others. Key Responsibilities: Provides leadership and management of the prospective student and applicant experience, including supervision of the Admissions Specialist and Graduate Assistants. Manages the office’s day-to-day operations, ensuring all inquiries receive timely and accurate follow-up communications. Provides guidance to staff on how to handle various applicant queries throughout the application cycle. Makes judgment calls on routine applicant issues and escalates complex cases to the Assistant Dean. Conducts one on one counseling sessions with prospective students, applicants and current students, advising on program details and funding opportunities. Ensures that the webpage and office communications share accurate and helpful financial aid and funding information. Works collaboratively with campus stakeholders including Academic Departments, Associate Deans, and Student Services to ensure students receive high quality, integrated services. Analyzes business processes and makes recommendations to improve efficiency of processes related to admissions and financial aid. Tracks budget and metrics and provides data and reporting/analysis related to financial aid. Makes decisions on the allocation of the Lynch School’s tuition remission budget, in conjunction with the Assistant Dean. Collaborates with the Dean’s Office and departments on doctoral student assistantship appointments, stipend payments, and tuition remission. Tracks, enters, and approves financial aid awards in the student system. Liaises with University Student Services on financial aid and billing matters. Serves as lead on a portfolio of virtual and in-person recruitment and yield events, and participates as a key contributor on others. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $74,450 to $93,050; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree or equivalent required. Master’s degree or advanced coursework in business, higher education management preferred. A minimum of three to five years of professional level admissions and/or marketing experience within higher education required. Strong organizational, prioritization and project management skills. Excellent verbal and written communication, interpersonal, and customer service skills. Skill in locating and reaching out to potential candidates and in increasing the diversity of the School. Enthusiasm for the mission of the Lynch School of Education and Human Development and excitement about the opportunity to promote the Lynch School to wider publics. Highly energetic and enthusiastic with the motivation to meet and exceed goals. Strong computer skills including MS Office and a thorough knowledge of social networking sites. Willingness and ability to learn new software programs and technologies quickly.  Working knowledge of Slate preferred. Cooperative team player who works well independently and with others in a team-oriented, fast-paced environment. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11187?c=bc": {
      "title": "Legal Information Librarian/Lecturer in Law",
      "description": "Legal Information Librarian/Lecturer in Law | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Legal Information Librarian/Lecturer in Law Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11187 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/27/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Boston College Law Library has an exciting opportunity for a Legal Information Librarian to join our team. The Legal Information Librarian participates as a member of the Teaching & Research (T&R) team. T&R operates as a self-managed team that promotes an atmosphere of teamwork, strategic action, respect, integrity, self-directed work, collegiality, and excellence. The department plays an essential role in providing research instruction and support to Boston College Law School students, faculty, and staff. Legal Information Librarians may teach popular credit-bearing legal research courses and offer additional instruction to ensure students have the necessary skills to be successful legal practitioners. The Legal Information Librarian also provides individualized support for faculty scholarship and classroom instruction. Responsibilities may change as they are dependent on the priorities of the law school, library and department needs. Responsibilities ●      Deliver legal research instruction to law students through credit-bearing research courses, guest lectures, library-sponsored research workshops and online learning resources. ●      Provide research and reference support to the Boston College Law School community. ●      Serve as liaison to assigned faculty members providing support for scholarship and teaching. ●      Contribute to the development of new library services and programs. ●      Participate in outreach activities promoting engagement within the law school community. ●      Identify and evaluate new resources through participation in the collection development process. ●      Serve on library, law school, and other professional committees. ●      Perform other duties as assigned. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $86,300 to $107,850; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements ●      J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school. ●      M.L.S. from an ALA-accredited institution. ●      Knowledge and experience conducting legal research and providing reference support. ●      Experience or interest in providing research instruction through a variety of modalities. ●      Interest in curriculum development, teaching, and assessment. ●      Ability to work as part of a team to solve problems, learn new skills, and support each other's work. ●      Strong written and oral communication skills. ●      Commitment to providing a high level of customer service. ●      Demonstrated ability to work flexibly with faculty, students, staff, and a diverse community. ●      Commitment to an inclusive learning environment. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11183?c=bc": {
      "title": "Interim BC bOp! Jazz Ensemble Conductor",
      "description": "Interim BC bOp! Jazz Ensemble Conductor | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Interim BC bOp! Jazz Ensemble Conductor Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11183 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/26/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Job Title: Interim BC bOp! Jazz Ensemble Conductor Position Type : Interim/Temporary | Nonexempt | Part-Time Appointment Period : August 31, 2026 - May 31, 2027 Location : Chestnut Hill, Massachusetts Compensation: $30-35/hour, commensurate with experience and education. Boston College seeks an experienced jazz musician and educator to serve as Interim BC bOp! Jazz Ensemble Conductor for the 2026–2027 academic year (September - May). Founded in 1987, BC bOp! is Boston College's instrumental and vocal jazz ensemble. The ensemble performs a diverse repertoire ranging from traditional to contemporary and Latin jazz, and provides students with opportunities to develop their musicianship, ensemble skills, and confidence in a supportive, collaborative environment. Reporting to the Director of the Robsham Theater Arts Center, the Conductor will provide musical and educational leadership for the ensemble, oversee rehearsals and performances, and support the development of its student musicians and vocalists. The Conductor will work closely with the BC bOp! Vocal Director, Boston College Bands staff, and student leaders. Key Responsibilities Conduct and direct regular rehearsals and performances for the instrumental and vocal ensembles. Select and prepare repertoire and provide instruction in jazz performance, ensemble technique, improvisation, interpretation, and musicianship. Prepare the ensemble for performances and schedule additional rehearsals as needed. Provide musical guidance and oversight for weekly sectionals, including vocal, rhythm, trumpet, trombone, and saxophone sections. Collaborate with the BC bOp! Vocal Director to support the development of the vocal ensemble. Foster a collaborative and engaging environment that supports student learning and development. Support student leadership and involvement in the artistic and operational aspects of the ensemble. Manage basic ensemble administration, including rosters, parts, rehearsal schedules, and communication with students. Coordinate with Boston College staff regarding facilities, scheduling, performances, and other operational needs. Support recruitment and retention of student musicians and vocalists. Participate in repertoire research, score preparation, planning meetings, and other activities necessary for the successful operation of the ensemble. Schedule The position requires regular availability for rehearsals, sectionals, preparation, and performances. Regular Rehearsals Instrumentalists: Tuesday and Thursday, 4:30–6:30 p.m. Additional Commitments Weekly one-hour sectionals for Saxophone, Trumpet, Trombone, and Rhythm sections, scheduled based on student and staff availability. Approximately five hours per week for rehearsal preparation, repertoire research, score preparation, and related work. Attendance at scheduled performances and additional rehearsals as needed. The annual performance schedule typically includes performances in October, December, February, and April, including the Holiday Concert, a major Robsham Theater concert, and Arts Fest performances. Applicants should submit: Current résumé or curriculum vitae; Brief cover letter describing their interest in the position and relevant experience; Examples of relevant teaching and/or professional performance experience; and Contact information for two or three professional references. Please note: Applicants will be asked to provide recordings or other examples of relevant musical work during the interview process. Boston College is committed to creating a supportive learning and working environment. We encourage applications from candidates who will contribute to student development, the educational mission, and Boston College's musical community. Requirements Required Demonstrated experience directing, conducting, or leading jazz ensembles. Significant experience performing and/or teaching jazz, with strong knowledge of jazz repertoire and performance practices. Ability to effectively rehearse and prepare a jazz ensemble for performance. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with college students in an educational and performance setting. Strong interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills. Bachelor's degree in music, music education, jazz studies, or a related field, or equivalent professional experience. Preferred Master's degree in music, jazz studies, music education, or a related field. Professional experience as a jazz instrumentalist, vocalist, arranger, conductor, or bandleader. Experience working with multiple sections of a jazz ensemble, including rhythm, saxophone, trumpet, trombone, and vocal sections. Experience working with college or university jazz ensembles. Closing Statement Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
