    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11349?c=bc": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Drug Delivery and Tissue Engineering",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Drug Delivery and Tissue Engineering | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Drug Delivery and Tissue Engineering Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11349 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Job Title: Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department: Engineering Employment Type: Onsite | Temporary | Full-Time (35 hours per week) FTE Range: $67,000 - $76,962, based on experience. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to lead projects in drug delivery and plant-based scaffolds for tissue engineering. Our lab is interested in (1) developing stimuli-responsive drug carriers for cancer and tissue engineering applications and (2) engineering the degradation of plant-based scaffolds for hard tissue engineering, as humans lack the enzymes to break down lignocellulosic plant structures during tissue regeneration. For this research, we utilize drug delivery, plant decellularization, tissue engineering, medical imaging (ultrasound and micro-CT), and AI/machine learning approaches. The successful candidate will lead project design, coordination, and experimental execution, providing leadership in project direction. There will be opportunities to work closely with and mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers. The Fellow will also have career development opportunities, including support for conference travel, grant writing, and mentorship from multidisciplinary faculty involved in the projects. Key Responsibilities: Planning, designing, and conducting experiments Analyzing and interpreting data in the context of project goals Maintaining reproducible, well-documented experimental protocols and analysis pipelines Collaborating with multidisciplinary project teams Contributing to manuscript preparation, conference presentations, proposal development, and publication of high-quality research papers Managing inventory for reagents and consumables Mentoring undergraduate, graduate, and other research assistants This is a full-time, one-year appointment, renewable for additional years based on performance and funding availability. We are looking to fill this position as soon as possible but will remain flexible on the specific start date for the right candidate. To apply for this position, please submit the following: a cover letter outlining research interests and fit for the position, a CV, and the names and contact information for three referees (contacted only for shortlisted candidates). For additional information, please contact Dr. Ali Salifu (ali.salifu@bc.edu). Requirements PhD in Biomedical Engineering or a related field Strong research background in drug delivery and tissue engineering Record of high-quality research publications Excellent teamwork, organizational, communication, and mentoring skills, including genuine interest in mentoring undergraduate researchers. Candidates with experience in AI/machine learning approaches to drug delivery and tissue engineering are especially encouraged to apply. Familiarity with decellularized scaffolds and omics techniques (transcriptomics, proteomics, and metabolomics) is a plus. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11347?c=bc": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in AI for Medical Ultrasound Image Analysis",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in AI for Medical Ultrasound Image Analysis | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Postdoctoral Research Fellow in AI for Medical Ultrasound Image Analysis Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11347 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Job Title: Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department: Engineering Employment Type: Onsite | Full-Time (35 Hours per week) | Temporary FTE Range: $67,000 - $76,962 based on experience Postdoctoral Research Fellow in AI for Medical Ultrasound Image Analysis The Biomedical Imaging and Instrumentation Lab in the Department of Engineering at Boston College (bc.edu/ranger-lab), led by Prof. Bryan Ranger, is recruiting a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join a multi-site research project funded by an NIH R01 award from the Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (NICHD), developing AI-enabled portable ultrasound methods to assess body composition and growth in preterm infants. This is an exciting opportunity for a postdoctoral researcher who wants to do meaningful, clinically grounded work in medical imaging AI. This position focuses on leading the analytic and machine learning components of a longitudinal, multi-site study following preterm infants from birth through 24 months. The fellow will work at the intersection of AI/ML, medical imaging, and clinical neonatal research. They will develop predictive models that integrate ultrasound imaging with clinical and maternal data, in close collaboration with clinical investigators at Brigham and Women's Hospital, the University of Minnesota Masonic Children's Hospital, and the Boston College Connell School of Nursing (CSON), as well as biostatisticians at Boston College. The fellow will be co-mentored by Dr. Ranger and Dr. Yuan Yuan (Computer Science, Boston College). Why Join This Project Direct clinical collaboration: you’ll work closely with colleagues at the Boston College CSON and with pediatricians at Brigham and Women's Hospital and the University of Minnesota Masonic Children's Hospital. Opportunities to develop novel machine learning methods: you’ll work with real-world clinical imaging data to devise and validate novel predictive models, from foundation model adaptation to longitudinal trajectory analysis. Leadership opportunities: you’ll take primary responsibility for analytic strategy and model development, with genuine input into the direction of the project's aims as it evolves. You will also work closely and have opportunities to mentor graduate and undergraduate student researchers. Career development: support for conference travel, opportunity to lead research and be first-author on publications across both the AI and clinical literatures, and direct experience with proposal writing and grant management. A collaborative, multi-disciplinary, mentorship-focused lab: you'll be co-mentored by faculty in engineering and computer science, with a team that values your growth as a researcher and mentor. Key Responsibilities Lead data analysis and predictive modeling efforts that integrate ultrasound, clinical, and maternal data to develop and validate models for body composition and growth outcomes across two clinical sites. Apply advanced AI/ML methods, including computer vision for real-time scan quality feedback, longitudinal trajectory modeling, and uncertainty quantification, in collaboration with the project's biostatistician. Maintain reproducible, well-documented analysis pipelines and code for public release. Supervise and mentor graduate and undergraduate research students. Interpret results in the context of the study's clinical aims, and work closely with clinical investigators to ensure rigor and clinical relevance. Contribute to manuscript preparation, conference presentations, and proposal development. This is a full-time, one-year appointment, renewable for additional years based on performance and funding availability. We are looking to fill this position as soon as possible but will remain flexible on specific start date for the right candidate. Applicants should submit: A cover letter describing research interests and fit for the position. A curriculum vitae (CV). Names and contact information of two references (contacted only for shortlisted candidates). If you are shortlisted for the position, you will be contacted for a virtual interview. For inquiries, please contact Prof. Bryan Ranger (bryan.ranger@bc.edu). Requirements Requirements Ph.D. in Computer Science, Biomedical Engineering, or a related field. Strong research background in machine learning, computer vision, or medical image analysis. Experience working with clinical or healthcare data preferred; familiarity with longitudinal or multimodal data integration a plus. Demonstrated research excellence through publications in relevant AI/ML or biomedical imaging venues. Excellent teamwork, communication, and mentoring skills, including genuine interest in mentoring undergraduate researchers. Ability to work effectively across engineering/computational and clinical disciplines. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11346?c=bc": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Director, Leadership Giving",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Director, Leadership Giving | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate Director, Leadership Giving Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11346 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description As we enter the fourth year of the public phase of Soaring Higher: the Campaign for Boston College, we invite you to join our community. Driven by our mission in ways that advance the university for years to come, priority areas for this $3B comprehensive campaign include Financial Aid, Academics, and Student Life. Based on a fundamental Jesuit tenet of developing the whole person, University Advancement is large enough to benefit from many campus resources, yet small enough to be agile and creative in supporting all associates. With an average tenure of close to 10 years at Boston College, our 250-person centralized organizational structure offers a variety of roles across fundraising, alumni relations, operations, and communications and engagement. We invest in the professional growth of every staff member through individual and team-based programs. Likewise, we aim to attract the very best, seeking team members who are as ambitious and as committed to our team as we are. We value a culture of diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging while actively promoting team members who contribute to the success of University Advancement. See below the current opportunity to begin (or return to!) your Boston College experience! Job Description: The Assistant/Associate Director, Leadership Giving reports to the Senior Associate Director of Leadership Giving and is responsible for identifying, soliciting, and stewarding gifts ranging from $10K through $249K from Boston College alumni, parents, and friends. The Assistant/Associate Director manages a portfolio of roughly 150 regional prospects (region to be determined) and is expected to meet specific goals surrounding meetings, solicitations, and new commitments. Assistant/Associate Directors on the Leadership Giving team are responsible for conducting 140-150 meetings a year and raising $800K to $1M per year. The Assistant/Associate Director writes effective solicitation letters and proposals and works collaboratively with peers in other fundraising areas including gift planning, athletics development, school development, and organizational giving to maximize fundraising productivity. In addition, the Assistant/Associate Director works with the Senior Associate Director to develop and implement a comprehensive strategy to increase annual cash giving through the solicitation, recognition, and stewardship of annual leadership donors. The Assistant/Associate Director assists in the functional supervision of one development assistant. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The salary range for this position is listed below, with the final salary based on various factors, including the candidate’s qualifications, skills, competencies, experience, and internal equity considerations. Title and salary commensurate with relevant experience based on the following titles and full-time equivalent hiring ranges: - Assistant Director: $80,100 - $100,150; 1-3 years experience - Associate Director: $90,600 - $113,250; 3+ years related professional experience Requirements This position requires a Bachelor’s degree and a minimum of 1-3 years of professional level frontline fundraising experience for the Assistant Director level, and 3-5 years at the Associate Director level. A proven record of soliciting and closing annual cash gifts of $10,000 - $25,000 at the Assistant Director level and $25,000 or more at the Associate Director level, preferably in a higher education environment, is required. Determination of job level and compensation will be commensurate with experience. Travel and some evenings and weekend work is required to be successful.  A valid driver’s license is required.  A demonstrated ability to speak and write persuasively about higher education and the Jesuit tradition is expected.  Proficiency with technology and relevant software applications, such as Google mail (Gmail), MS Word, MS Excel, and PowerPoint is expected. Must be a team player who is highly motivated and dedicated to the goals of advancing higher education and have an appreciation for Jesuit Catholic education and the goals and values of Boston College. The office of University Advancement supports the mission of Boston College by promoting a culture of inclusion that values each individual. Our recruiting practices support that commitment to build teams reflecting our community; students, alumni, parents and friends of Boston College. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11327?c=bc": {
      "title": "Third Cook - Newton",
      "description": "Third Cook - Newton | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Third Cook - Newton Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11327 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/19/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description Third Cook 37.5 hours per week / 52 weeks per year Basic Function and Responsibility: Under direction, performs a variety of routine, supportive duties to assist in the preparation of food for use in the dining operations and/or catered events. Moderately heavy lifting is necessary. Characteristic Duties: As assigned, assists in the preparation of food items using recipes for all meals (breakfast, lunch, receptions and dinner). As assigned, provides routine support in kitchen by performing duties such as, slicing, mixing, grilling, sauteing, roasting, steaming, baking and stewing. Under direction of individual in charge, monitors foods while cooking to maintain proper temperatures and ensure proper timing cycles. Transports food to serving areas, assembles and replenish food items as required. May transport food and equipment to catered functions and then assists in the execution of events as assigned. Performs routine sanitation and safety functions of work areas and equipment. May assist in the training of food service workers and students as directed. May be assigned other duties during slow periods such as semester breaks, holidays and summer. Performs all other reasonable duties as assigned. Supervision Received: Reports to the Chef Manager in assigned unit, while receiving functional direction from unit chef, first, cook and other support personnel. Supervision Exercised: May assist in the monitoring of work by food service workers and students as required. Hiring Range: $25.78/hr to $28.65/hr. Pay rate commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements High School diploma or equivalent.  Additional relevant experience may substitute for education. NRA ServSafe certification and MRA allergen training required. Minimum of 1 year experience in a large volume food operation. Must be able to read and write English. The ability to work effectively in a fast-paced operation with diverse staff. Outstanding human relations skills. Must be able to operate basic kitchen equipment. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    },
    "https://bc.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/11325?c=bc": {
      "title": "Director, Dining Services",
      "description": "Director, Dining Services | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Director, Dining Services Chestnut Hill, MA, United States | req11325 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/16/2026 Back to Search Boston College Introduction Founded in 1863, Boston College is a Jesuit, Catholic university located six miles from downtown Boston with an enrollment of 9,654 full-time undergraduates and 5,072 graduate and professional students. Ranked 37 among national universities, Boston College has 923 full-time and 1,336 FTE faculty, 2,822 non-faculty employees, an operating budget of $1.4 billion, and an endowment in excess of $3.9 billion. Job Description The Director, Dining Services reports to the Executive Director, Auxiliary\nServices and is responsible for recommending\ninnovative ideas as well as implementing and evaluating strategies and policies\nthat will ensure the University attains its objectives related to Dining’s revenue targets and\nestablished financial goals. Boston College Dining is a\nself-operated dining program with an annual operating budget of approximately\n$60 million, 14 dining locations on 4\ncampuses and a staff of more than 450 employees.  The Director is a\ncreative, dynamic and collaborative leader that oversees those operational\nactivities that includes discretionary and retail meal plans,\nand financial operations plus extensive on and off premise catering, and self-operated\nAthletic Concessions across a number of sports and locations. Key responsibilities include: Collaborate with relevant University stakeholders, develop and implement Dining’s strategic plan and goals. Provide mentoring, coaching, direction and leadership support to team members in order to achieve desired business and customer service results. Manage staffing levels for appropriate levels of business and consistent with forecasting. Develop food service, customer service, and production policies and procedures and oversee their implementation. Provide tactical oversight for all food service operations on 4 Boston College campuses and facilitate the prioritization process and monitor new activities to ensure proper allocation of resources in support of overall business objectives. Ensure achievement of high standards for the development and implementation of quality assurance policies and procedures related to food quality, sanitation, equipment maintenance, safety, customer service, etc. Accountable for the financial management of the Dining operations and works in collaboration with the Auxiliary Services financial team to develop and manage the annual Dining operating and capital budgets, establish food and labor cost goals and also provide support to the Dining management team with the development of unit-specific operating budgets. Lead\nthe public relations program including social media and external communications,\nand manage and resolve inquiries from students, parents and community members. Full-Time Equivalent Hiring Range: $167,800 to $209,750; salary commensurate with relevant experience. Requirements Bachelor’s degree in business, hospitality management or a\nrelated field; or equivalent combination of education and experience.  Master’s degree preferred.  At least ten years\nof senior level experience and responsibility, preferably in a complex\nenterprise environment of a not for profit institution.  To be successful, must have proven\nsupervisory and financial management abilities to effectively accomplish the\nUniversity's Dining objectives and to support the overall goals of the\nUniversity. Excellent planning skills for creating and implementing\ncost effective meal plan programs, merchandising and marketing programs, and\nhuman resource development. Exceptional oral and written communication skills to\ninteract effectively, at varying levels, with a community of employees,\nstudents, faculty, staff, visitors and customers. Highly\ncompetent at simplifying complex situations and negotiating successfully in an environment\nwith multiple stakeholders. Proven\nexperience and success in developing and managing budgets with ability to\nanalyze reports, metrics and trends. Closing Statement Boston College offers a broad and competitive range of benefits depending on your job classification eligibility: Tuition remission for Employees Tuition remission for Spouses and Children who meet eligibility requirements Generous Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Low-Cost Life Insurance Eligibility for both University-Funded 401k and Employer-Sponsored 403b Retirement Plans Paid Holidays Annually Generous Sick and Vacation Pay Additional benefits can be found on https://www.bc.edu/employeehandbook Boston College conducts pre-employment background checks as part of the hiring process. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category, including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination . Apply Now Share Save Job As an applicant for employment, you are entitled to a copy of Boston College’s Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report, located on the Campus Security Information Page . This web page contains Boston College’s campus safety program, which includes the University’s safety policies and campus crime statistics reported during the previous three years, as well as the University’s fire safety program. To request a paper copy of this report, please contact the Boston College Police Department at 617-552-4446. Boston College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate on the basis of any legally protected category including disability and protected veteran status. Boston College's Notice of Nondiscrimination can be viewed at https://www.bc.edu/nondiscrimination ."
    }
  },
